WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:16.450
Tim Ferriss: Stephen, it's nice to see you.

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:17.780
Thank you for making the time today.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.420
Stephen Wolfram: Nice to be here.

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:28.490
Tim Ferriss: I was impressed that just before
we started recording, when I fumbled an attempt

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:35.300
to recall when it was that we sat next to
each other, you very quickly said 2011 Wired

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.960
Health Conference and mentioned that you have
an archive.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:42.130
So how do you search for something like that?

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:46.020
Stephen Wolfram: I keep an awful lot of stuff.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:49.129
I've got all my email going back 30 years.

00:00:49.129 --> 00:00:54.449
I have a habit of writing trip reports for
myself whenever I go to some event or something

00:00:54.449 --> 00:00:55.579
like that.

00:00:55.579 --> 00:00:59.790
And I have also scans of paper documents.

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:00.899
You weren't a paper document.

00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:04.110
You're more recent than paper document, so
to speak.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:08.860
But I have scans of, well it's like a quarter
million pages or so, paper documents that

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:13.369
I generated in the time in my life before
I went fully digital, so to speak.

00:01:13.369 --> 00:01:15.270
And I also tend to record.

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:19.909
I record all the key strokes I type and screen
captures and all kinds of things like that.

00:01:19.909 --> 00:01:20.984
And then —
Tim Ferriss: How would you use — I'm sorry

00:01:20.984 --> 00:01:24.040
to — I was just going to ask, how do you
use the logging of the key strokes?

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:25.040
How would that be used?

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.040
Stephen Wolfram: I don't usually.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:27.040
Tim Ferriss: You do not.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.590
Stephen Wolfram: Usually I don't.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:33.509
Occasionally I'll want to know — occasionally,
for example, some computer will crash in some

00:01:33.509 --> 00:01:36.510
horrible way and it'll be like, oh, I just
lost a bunch of stuff.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.060
Well, no I didn't, because I had it recorded.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.060
Tim Ferriss: Ah.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:40.060
Right.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:43.040
Stephen Wolfram: That was the thing that caused
me like 25 years ago to start recording those,

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:44.399
is one crash.

00:01:44.399 --> 00:01:48.100
And then I decided it's cheap to just record
everything like that.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.149
And occasionally I'll do things like, oh,
I'm using a new keyboard.

00:01:51.149 --> 00:01:54.719
Do I type faster or slower on my new keyboard?

00:01:54.719 --> 00:01:55.719
Those kinds of things.

00:01:55.719 --> 00:01:56.719
Tim Ferriss: Got it.

00:01:56.719 --> 00:01:57.719
And you can track that type of thing.

00:01:57.719 --> 00:01:59.549
Stephen Wolfram: It's easy to answer that
question if you have that data.

00:01:59.549 --> 00:02:05.289
Tim Ferriss: It's one thing to record or ingest
information, it's quite another to structure

00:02:05.289 --> 00:02:06.590
your thinking.

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:15.080
And I, in the process of doing research for
this conversation, came across some discussion

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.819
on Reddit involving creating matrices.

00:02:18.819 --> 00:02:24.120
So I wanted to explore your use of matrices
for different projects.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:31.629
I'm just going to read a very, very short
section here, which reads, "I actively avoid

00:02:31.629 --> 00:02:33.629
thinking about things where I don't have a
'matrix.'

00:02:33.629 --> 00:02:36.920
I don't like to have 'disembodied ideas' floating
around."

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:39.709
Which is my current state of affairs, so I'm
selfishly asking this.

00:02:39.709 --> 00:02:44.069
"Of course, when something is important enough
to me, I try to build a 'matrix' for it."

00:02:44.069 --> 00:02:47.340
Could you give an example of what such a matrix
might look like?

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:48.340
Stephen Wolfram: Yeah.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:53.569
What I mean by that is, some kind of framework
in which I'm doing something.

00:02:53.569 --> 00:02:59.909
So for example, if I have a small idea about
molecular biology, I don't really have a good

00:02:59.909 --> 00:03:01.730
place to put that idea.

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:06.209
If I'm doing a big project about molecular
biology where I'm building up a whole structure,

00:03:06.209 --> 00:03:08.519
then I have a place to put that.

00:03:08.519 --> 00:03:10.420
So it's that kind of thing.

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:17.290
I've been lucky enough, my main life work
is building our computational language, Wolfram

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:23.620
Language, which is this language that's supposed
to represent everything in the world computationally.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:28.110
So a large number of ideas that I have about
how to represent things computationally can

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:31.580
wind up in the matrix, which is the Wolfram
Language.

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:37.819
Similarly, I've found, for example, writing
my blog-like, kind of writings-thing, that's

00:03:37.819 --> 00:03:43.180
another kind of matrix into which I can put,
when I do historical studies of things, I'll

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:46.860
write a piece about that historical study
and that's a place to put it.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:47.860
Tim Ferriss: I see.

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:51.770
Stephen Wolfram: But when it's too small a
thing, I just don't have a place to put it,

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:53.819
and it tends to die on the vine.

00:03:53.819 --> 00:03:59.970
Since you're asking about this, I've just
been exposing myself to a very bizarre experience.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:04.599
Which is, I've just been finishing a project
that I started 50 years ago.

00:04:04.599 --> 00:04:05.890
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:09.980
Stephen Wolfram: And in the process of doing
this — something that I started, I got interested

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:16.320
in when I was 12 years old, it's a question
about physics and about the second law of

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:21.000
thermodynamics and why randomness gets generated
in the world and so on.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:26.360
I've made various pieces of progress on this
question over the years, but finally now with

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:30.669
a bunch of the things that we've done recently
in understanding fundamental theory of physics,

00:04:30.669 --> 00:04:32.670
I think I can actually really nail this question.

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:38.419
So I've written a whole thing about the scientific
answer to that question, but then I thought

00:04:38.419 --> 00:04:44.840
I should write a piece describing my 50-year
journey of trying to answer this question.

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:51.289
And so that got me back into, I'm looking
at all my calendars from 1983, and I'm looking

00:04:51.289 --> 00:04:55.150
at all of these paper documents that I have
scanned and so on.

00:04:55.150 --> 00:05:00.169
It's a very interesting experience, going
back and seeing what mattered from 1983 and

00:05:00.169 --> 00:05:01.530
what didn't.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:05.479
How did I get to the things that were important
in the end?

00:05:05.479 --> 00:05:08.030
How did those come to be?

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:09.820
What were the kinds of steps that I had to
go through?

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:16.260
One of the things I really noticed, really
striking to me, is there's often a large amount

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:20.250
of time in which I was building some conceptual
framework for something.

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:24.710
Sometimes I had clues about how that framework
should work, I didn't even recognize the clues.

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:26.640
Just didn't understand it.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:31.819
Finally, through the very slow process, I
build this intellectual framework, and then

00:05:31.819 --> 00:05:32.819
I get some other clue.

00:05:32.819 --> 00:05:35.240
I do some other computer experiments, something
else happens.

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:38.850
And then literally I can trace, because I
have all the file creation dates, all this

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:40.740
kind of thing, I can trace.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:45.810
It was 15 minutes from the time when I saw
this to the time when I started writing this

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:46.810
and so on.

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:52.330
It's really remarkable how much years can
go by, but one's slowly building up the kind

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:57.611
of conceptual framework needed, and then it's
often very sudden to take the next step.

00:05:57.611 --> 00:05:59.819
That was a striking thing to me.

00:05:59.819 --> 00:06:04.660
Tim Ferriss: I want to ask a few follow up
questions about conceptual frameworks and

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:09.931
perhaps just request an example of what such
a conceptual framework might look like, for

00:06:09.931 --> 00:06:11.700
people listening and for me, frankly.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:17.510
But before we get to that, putting together
a blog post or an article that chronicles

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:24.570
your search for answers or exploration of
these open questions is quite an undertaking.

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:29.840
Reviewing calendars from 1983 and so on, I
would imagine requires a good amount of time.

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:31.050
Why do you do it?

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:34.200
Or is it the reward in the process of writing?

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:36.449
Is it something gratifying in the process
of writing?

00:06:36.449 --> 00:06:41.229
Are you hoping to impart something to those
who read this piece?

00:06:41.229 --> 00:06:42.229
Why do that?

00:06:42.229 --> 00:06:43.229
Stephen Wolfram: That's a good question.

00:06:43.229 --> 00:06:44.229
I was wondering it myself.

00:06:44.229 --> 00:06:47.229
I thought it would be really easy, but it
wasn't quite so easy.

00:06:47.229 --> 00:06:48.229
No.

00:06:48.229 --> 00:06:52.990
I have done quite a lot of historical biography,
usually of other people.

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:58.690
I find that when I'm really trying to understand
an idea, I need to know where that idea came

00:06:58.690 --> 00:06:59.690
from.

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:03.479
So for example, the things I'm doing right
now on the second law of thermodynamics thing,

00:07:03.479 --> 00:07:07.350
second law of thermodynamics was developed
in the 1860s.

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:11.400
I think people took a wrong turn sometime
shortly thereafter.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:14.930
So I think the thing that I've now figured
out is a little different from what people

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:16.699
had figured out at that time.

00:07:16.699 --> 00:07:20.669
And so when I'm saying I think they took a
wrong turn, I really want to know how did

00:07:20.669 --> 00:07:22.400
they come to take that wrong turn?

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:26.729
And so that's my next project for the next
week or so is going back and I collected the

00:07:26.729 --> 00:07:27.729
material for it.

00:07:27.729 --> 00:07:32.820
I get to go back and read all the original
sources of how people came to think about

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:33.820
those things.

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:38.750
But I don't feel confident that I know what
was going on until I can trace this person

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:43.690
took that move because they thought this and
they understood that and so on.

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:48.000
And I thought for myself, I was mostly, well,
a bit curious.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.520
I thought it was sort of an interesting story.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:56.430
It's a very rare case in history of science
that one actually has really precise, detailed

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:59.050
data on how some idea got developed.

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:03.819
And so I thought it's kind of an interesting
example of essentially computational history.

00:08:03.819 --> 00:08:06.970
One thinks about computational X for all X.

00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:08.900
This is computational history.

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:11.580
And it's like what can you do in computational
history?

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:16.000
What kinds of things can you expose in computational
history?

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:19.351
The other thing that's interesting to me,
is when I go back and think about things that

00:08:19.351 --> 00:08:25.990
I did or figured out 40 years ago, let's say,
I lived through it, but then when I looked

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:31.300
back I realized there were threads that could
be joined that I absolutely did not see at

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:32.300
the time.

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:34.710
Give you an example.

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:38.720
I was working on these simple programs.

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:40.580
What do simple programs do?

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:44.530
The big surprise was, even very simple programs
can do very complicated things.

00:08:44.530 --> 00:08:46.410
That was something I didn't expect.

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:48.220
It was a violation of my intuition.

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:53.260
It took me a couple of years to come to terms
with the fact that that was possible.

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:59.880
Same time, several of actually top mathematicians
who were friends of mine were trying to work

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:02.270
on the things that I had uncovered.

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:05.500
They said, "Let's go do math on these things.

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:08.040
Let's figure out more stuff about them."

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:09.840
And they worked for a while.

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:13.630
I just found a bunch of their notes, actually,
from going through these things from 1984

00:09:13.630 --> 00:09:14.630
and so on.

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:20.680
And they did a bunch of very sophisticated
math, and they couldn't figure out anything.

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:25.670
What I realized is, at the time I was just
like, okay, well I'm figuring out my things.

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:28.709
Their methods didn't happen to work, so what?

00:09:28.709 --> 00:09:35.920
What I then realized, just now, is the big
innovation of mine was realizing that the

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:40.790
fact that they couldn't figure out anything
was itself a super interesting fact.

00:09:40.790 --> 00:09:45.230
So in other words, there's this phenomenon
I call computational irreducibility, which

00:09:45.230 --> 00:09:50.470
is basically the big picture of why they couldn't
figure anything out.

00:09:50.470 --> 00:09:55.370
Usually you say, "I know the rules by which
some system operates."

00:09:55.370 --> 00:09:57.519
So then you might say, "Okay great, I've got
it nailed.

00:09:57.519 --> 00:09:59.649
I know everything about what's going to happen
in that system."

00:09:59.649 --> 00:10:02.460
Well, that's not true.

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:09.710
Because if the rules define some computation,
to know what the system does, you can find

00:10:09.710 --> 00:10:14.120
out by running the computation, the question
is can you outrun that computation?

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:19.490
Can you say, "Okay, system, you went through
a million steps of that computation, but I

00:10:19.490 --> 00:10:20.490
don't need to do that.

00:10:20.490 --> 00:10:21.990
I'm smarter than the system.

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:25.430
I can just say the answer is 42," or something.

00:10:25.430 --> 00:10:30.589
What computational irreducibility tells you,
is that in general you are stuck having to

00:10:30.589 --> 00:10:33.589
follow each step in the evolution of the system.

00:10:33.589 --> 00:10:36.850
And that's a really important fact about science.

00:10:36.850 --> 00:10:42.519
It's a way in which science explains from
within science that science has questions

00:10:42.519 --> 00:10:44.959
that it can't readily answer.

00:10:44.959 --> 00:10:50.279
The thing that I realized only, I don't know
what, 35 years after the fact, is that, in

00:10:50.279 --> 00:10:55.911
a sense, if I had an achievement, intellectual
achievement in that whole process, it was

00:10:55.911 --> 00:11:01.339
realizing that the fact that one had gotten
stuck was itself the most important thing

00:11:01.339 --> 00:11:02.339
to know.

00:11:02.339 --> 00:11:04.000
Not, "Oh, we got stuck.

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:05.160
Let's give up."

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:09.900
But the fact that one got stuck meant that
there was a paradigmatic change that had to

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:13.550
be made in the way that you think about these
kinds of questions in science.

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:19.070
So that's an example that, for me, is quite
useful, to go back and see what really happened

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:21.510
there, what was really important there, which
I had not realized at the time.

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:24.250
Tim Ferriss: Makes me — I was just going
to say, it makes me think a bit of the Sherlock

00:11:24.250 --> 00:11:30.750
Holmes, the case of 
the dog that didn't bark in the night.

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:34.000
The term that I'd love for you just to elaborate
on a little bit, and I apologize this is going

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:38.310
to be a muggle question, but for people who
are non-technical, I'll put myself in that

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:45.660
audience, when you say, for instance, computational
history or computational X, how should someone

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:51.200
who may have fear around the term "computational"
think of that term?

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:52.480
Computational history as an example?

00:11:52.480 --> 00:12:00.070
Stephen Wolfram: We humans like to come up
with abstract, formal ways to describe things.

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:02.610
Language itself is an example of that.

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:07.779
We see things out there in the world and we
say, "That's a tree, that's a dog, that's

00:12:07.779 --> 00:12:08.850
whatever."

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:13.470
The fact that we are able to symbolically
describe these things in the world — there

00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:15.990
are many different kinds of dogs and many
different details, but we just say "It's a

00:12:15.990 --> 00:12:21.830
dog" — so it's a way of organizing the things
that we see, in that case, just using natural

00:12:21.830 --> 00:12:22.830
language.

00:12:22.830 --> 00:12:27.300
There've been in history a variety of kinds
of organizational approaches.

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:31.660
Logic, for example, was one from antiquity.

00:12:31.660 --> 00:12:36.690
Mathematics is another kind of organizational
approach to say, "This is how you structure

00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:38.399
the way that you talk about the world."

00:12:38.399 --> 00:12:42.680
As far as I'm concerned, the importance of
computation is, it's another way to structure

00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:44.449
how you talk about the world.

00:12:44.449 --> 00:12:49.000
And a big part of what I've spent my life
doing is building this kind of computational

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:56.149
language, which provides a precise way to
take something like, I don't know, some description

00:12:56.149 --> 00:13:01.709
of some piece of food or some description
of some position on the Earth or whatever

00:13:01.709 --> 00:13:09.180
else, and represent those things computationally
in a precise way that has the feature that,

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:12.180
well a human could read it and say, "Oh, I
know what that means."

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:17.620
But also we have the extra boost from the
fact that a computer can read it too, and

00:13:17.620 --> 00:13:21.709
then the computer can help us to get further
with that.

00:13:21.709 --> 00:13:27.540
I mean, in a sense, one of the big things
that we, as a species, you know, big achievement

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:29.420
is human language.

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:35.340
You can take of things about the world and
describe them in a somewhat precise, abstract

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:36.340
way.

00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:41.649
I see computational language as being another
level in that evolution, except that we get

00:13:41.649 --> 00:13:47.200
to share the burden of seeing what happens,
not just with other humans, but with computers.

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:51.360
And so, for me, that's the big thing is describing
the world computationally.

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:57.130
Now, when I talk about simple programs and
those kinds of things, what I tend to mean

00:13:57.130 --> 00:13:59.550
is kind of a meta model of the world.

00:13:59.550 --> 00:14:05.760
So there are models of actual trees and dogs
and trajectories on the Earth and things like

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:06.760
this.

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:12.209
And then how do you break that down to something
even more primitive?

00:14:12.209 --> 00:14:18.120
And so then what you end up with are these
sets of rules that say, well, you could describe

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:20.000
what they are about in many different ways.

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:25.680
But for example, one type that I've studied
a lot, the technical name is cellular automata,

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:31.560
and the typical setup is you have a row of
cells, and each cell can be either black or

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:33.040
white, let's say.

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:39.540
And then the computational rule is, you look
at every cell and you say, what is the color

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:42.750
of that cell and its two neighbors, let's
say.

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:47.740
Based on that you say, okay, I'm going to
change the color of the cell to be white or

00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:48.949
to be black or whatever.

00:14:48.949 --> 00:14:52.670
You just keep running that rule over and over
again.

00:14:52.670 --> 00:14:58.259
The big surprise is, and this is the thing
I finally discovered in around 1984, the big

00:14:58.259 --> 00:15:04.050
surprise is that even with a rule that simple,
you can just start it off with one black dot

00:15:04.050 --> 00:15:06.220
and it makes this incredibly complicated pattern.

00:15:06.220 --> 00:15:11.560
A pattern so complicated that if you were
to just look at a piece of the pattern and

00:15:11.560 --> 00:15:16.180
say, is this random or is there some regularity
to it?

00:15:16.180 --> 00:15:18.779
You would just say, it looks completely random
to me.

00:15:18.779 --> 00:15:23.160
Even though the rule that made it is this
very, very simple rule that you can easily

00:15:23.160 --> 00:15:27.370
describe or write down or feed to a computer
or whatever else.

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:33.569
So for me, this notion of computation is having
this way to structure the way that you talk

00:15:33.569 --> 00:15:39.560
about the world, and then there's this kind
of meta modeling of that, which is to say

00:15:39.560 --> 00:15:43.860
what are the very simplest elements of that
computational process?

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:46.230
And then talk about what one can do with those.

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:52.810
I mean, I think a good analogy perhaps for
the computational description of the world

00:15:52.810 --> 00:15:57.050
comes from the mathematical notation that
one uses to talk about mathematics.

00:15:57.050 --> 00:16:02.130
I mean, it's sort of an interesting evolution
that if you look at mathematics done in antiquity

00:16:02.130 --> 00:16:06.470
and things like that, people didn't have a
symbol for plus.

00:16:06.470 --> 00:16:08.230
They just used words.

00:16:08.230 --> 00:16:13.790
And then some time around 500 years ago, people
started inventing a plus sign, an equals sign,

00:16:13.790 --> 00:16:14.930
things like this.

00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:20.610
And it's when that sort of streamlining of
the way to talk about math came online, that's

00:16:20.610 --> 00:16:24.870
where math really took off and algebra got
invented and then calculus and then we have

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:28.649
this whole mathematical approach to science
that was able to be done.

00:16:28.649 --> 00:16:37.760
I guess my own personal last 40 years of effort
has been to try to make a computational notation

00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:43.290
for talking about the world that is kind of
a parallel for computation of what mathematical

00:16:43.290 --> 00:16:46.050
notation is for the mathematical way of talking
about the world.

00:16:46.050 --> 00:16:50.319
Tim Ferriss: I have a question about natural
languages.

00:16:50.319 --> 00:16:56.519
I don't think I'm misquoting, but feel free
to fact check this.

00:16:56.519 --> 00:17:07.569
You are so deeply aware of and able to work
with language in multiple, I would say, dimensions.

00:17:07.569 --> 00:17:15.010
I did read at one point you were considering
a job with CERN and I believe I read that

00:17:15.010 --> 00:17:18.720
you said you had practiced French but had
never built up the nerve to use it, or something

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:20.199
along those lines.

00:17:20.199 --> 00:17:22.429
And I don't know if that was the tongue-in-cheek
comment, but —

00:17:22.429 --> 00:17:24.049
Stephen Wolfram: Well no, no.

00:17:24.049 --> 00:17:25.680
That's from ancient times.

00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:29.380
I went to fancy schools in England when I
was growing up.

00:17:29.380 --> 00:17:32.929
I learned three languages, Latin, Greek, and
French.

00:17:32.929 --> 00:17:39.190
Okay, you don't get to speak Latin or Greek,
ancient Greek, at least not in most places.

00:17:39.190 --> 00:17:41.640
But French, you could in principle speak.

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:45.010
I can read scientific French pretty fluently.

00:17:45.010 --> 00:17:50.670
But if you say — if I'm in France and I'm
like, "Can I order that piece of food or something?"

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:51.670
No way.

00:17:51.670 --> 00:17:53.720
I can't do that.

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:54.720
It's one of these things.

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:55.950
I should get over it one day.

00:17:55.950 --> 00:17:59.840
Because I think I have the vocabulary knowledge,
I think, and all that.

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:01.770
I just have never really gotten into that.

00:18:01.770 --> 00:18:04.929
I've been very deeply involved in computational
language.

00:18:04.929 --> 00:18:10.600
I've not been deeply — I've been interested
in human language, but I'm not from the point

00:18:10.600 --> 00:18:14.559
of view of the practice of learning lots of
human languages.

00:18:14.559 --> 00:18:19.540
It's one of those skills where I could have
put a lot of effort into it, but it’s like,

00:18:19.540 --> 00:18:23.200
automatic translation is now getting to the
point where, for many kinds of things, not

00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:24.640
so important anymore.

00:18:24.640 --> 00:18:28.590
Just like I could have been a champion map
reader.

00:18:28.590 --> 00:18:32.559
I'm glad I didn't put huge effort into that,
because I just use a GPS now.

00:18:32.559 --> 00:18:34.110
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that's true.

00:18:34.110 --> 00:18:39.100
I suppose I am the opposite in the sense that
I've spent a lot of time on natural languages,

00:18:39.100 --> 00:18:47.450
in part because I derive so much pleasure
and I think cognitive exercise from pursuing

00:18:47.450 --> 00:18:54.280
it, but looking at the progression, say, in
Google Translate from my last trip to Japan,

00:18:54.280 --> 00:19:00.570
which was pre-COVID, to just about six weeks
ago, it is astonishing —

00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:02.470
Stephen Wolfram: That's interesting.

00:19:02.470 --> 00:19:05.360
Tim Ferriss: — how much it has improved.

00:19:05.360 --> 00:19:10.490
Fortunately, I already speak, read, and write
Japanese reasonably well, because I went there

00:19:10.490 --> 00:19:16.710
as an exchange student when I was 15, but
the extent to which someone can now use their

00:19:16.710 --> 00:19:21.740
voice pretty synchronously to communicate
with someone with automatic translation is

00:19:21.740 --> 00:19:22.740
remarkable.

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:30.390
Where do you see that, or how do you see that
developing, say, in the next handful of years

00:19:30.390 --> 00:19:31.390
or in the near term?

00:19:31.390 --> 00:19:35.590
I mean, this is something I would imagine
you probably have a view on.

00:19:35.590 --> 00:19:41.559
But how do you think we'll be using this type
of technology in the next, call it five years?

00:19:41.559 --> 00:19:49.000
Stephen Wolfram: I think one of the things
that it really drills into is the whole question

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:54.059
of can you actually express the same thoughts
in different human languages?

00:19:54.059 --> 00:19:56.330
And that's a deep issue.

00:19:56.330 --> 00:20:06.799
I think what we realize is that, language
is one representation of organized human thoughts.

00:20:06.799 --> 00:20:13.250
In a sense, it is a societal construction
of we all know what a chair is.

00:20:13.250 --> 00:20:17.820
So when we use the word "chair," we know what
each other is talking about.

00:20:17.820 --> 00:20:21.870
But if you have a language that comes from
a place where the environment, the culture

00:20:21.870 --> 00:20:27.030
is very different, you'll end up with words
where there really just isn't a translation

00:20:27.030 --> 00:20:28.520
for that word.

00:20:28.520 --> 00:20:32.730
Because there just isn't the shared cultural
understanding of what one's talking about

00:20:32.730 --> 00:20:33.730
there.

00:20:33.730 --> 00:20:40.740
So I think the thing that will be pretty interesting
to see is, as we see the tightening up of

00:20:40.740 --> 00:20:46.270
the structural aspects of translation, at
what point do we really realize, in that culture

00:20:46.270 --> 00:20:50.720
there are thoughts that we just don't have
in some other culture?

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:55.570
And that's something, as you start generalizing
that, it's like, okay, how do we communicate

00:20:55.570 --> 00:20:57.620
with the alien intelligences?

00:20:57.620 --> 00:20:59.730
How do you communicate with cats and dogs?

00:20:59.730 --> 00:21:01.900
How do you communicate with AIs?

00:21:01.900 --> 00:21:02.900
Things like this.

00:21:02.900 --> 00:21:08.480
These are all examples of alien intelligences
with which we share certain kinds of things.

00:21:08.480 --> 00:21:13.809
We share some emotional responses with pets
and things like this, but we don't share,

00:21:13.809 --> 00:21:19.669
probably, some sort of deep philosophical
convictions and so on.

00:21:19.669 --> 00:21:25.480
It's interesting to see how this process of
translation can work and how far out can you

00:21:25.480 --> 00:21:27.080
translate things.

00:21:27.080 --> 00:21:32.770
I guess for computers, the thing I've been
most involved in, is how do you go from the

00:21:32.770 --> 00:21:38.880
things we think about in our minds to the
things that we can represent for a computer?

00:21:38.880 --> 00:21:43.600
And how do we — computers can compute all
kinds of things.

00:21:43.600 --> 00:21:48.770
Many of the things they can compute, we humans
don't at least currently care about.

00:21:48.770 --> 00:21:54.179
There's a certain small set of possible things
computers can do that are things that relate

00:21:54.179 --> 00:21:58.500
to the things that we humans in the current
state of our civilization and so on have decided

00:21:58.500 --> 00:21:59.789
we care about.

00:21:59.789 --> 00:22:05.890
And so an interesting question to understand,
to what extent we can translate the things

00:22:05.890 --> 00:22:11.200
we think we care about into something which
can be represented computationally?

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:15.990
And it comes back again, I suppose after one's
spent one's life working on something, everything

00:22:15.990 --> 00:22:21.549
somehow relates to these questions like, how
do you make this computational language to

00:22:21.549 --> 00:22:25.150
represent human thoughts in a computational
way?

00:22:25.150 --> 00:22:30.340
Now, when you talk about natural language
translation and so on, what we've done when

00:22:30.340 --> 00:22:36.640
we make our Wolfram|Alpha system and intelligent
assistant uses of that and so on, what it's

00:22:36.640 --> 00:22:43.370
doing is it's taking a natural language question,
like what's the population of India divided

00:22:43.370 --> 00:22:46.470
by China in 1960 or something?

00:22:46.470 --> 00:22:56.000
And it's taking that and it is turning that
into a precise computational question, precise

00:22:56.000 --> 00:22:59.450
symbolic representation that we can then compute
the answer from.

00:22:59.450 --> 00:23:06.040
But whatever poetry there might have been
in that question, can you tell me the population

00:23:06.040 --> 00:23:10.789
of the, I don't know, some poetic name for
some country and some other thing.

00:23:10.789 --> 00:23:13.010
We crush all the poetry out of that.

00:23:13.010 --> 00:23:19.480
We're just turning it into, so what is the
precise computational representation that

00:23:19.480 --> 00:23:23.250
is good computer speak, so to speak.

00:23:23.250 --> 00:23:30.559
Whereas it could have been that the very appreciative
way that somebody described some country translated

00:23:30.559 --> 00:23:36.720
into some other human language, that notion
of appreciation would've been the most important

00:23:36.720 --> 00:23:40.530
part of that thing that one's asking.

00:23:40.530 --> 00:23:44.720
But for the computer it says, "I don't care
about that.

00:23:44.720 --> 00:23:49.470
I'm just here to provide a symbolic representation
and give the answer," so to speak.

00:23:49.470 --> 00:23:56.549
I think the thing to understand about translation,
ultimately, is the destination mind isn't

00:23:56.549 --> 00:24:04.070
built the same way the source mind is necessarily
built, and so there may just be no way to

00:24:04.070 --> 00:24:05.070
change that.

00:24:05.070 --> 00:24:06.070
Now, you know can see that.

00:24:06.070 --> 00:24:11.990
If you imagine you have two machine-learning
neuralnet systems and they've both been trained

00:24:11.990 --> 00:24:17.350
how to tell cats from dogs, for example, the
internal methods by which they will do it

00:24:17.350 --> 00:24:19.669
will typically be quite different.

00:24:19.669 --> 00:24:24.559
The details of how they will have reacted
to that training will be quite different.

00:24:24.559 --> 00:24:28.790
And so there isn't a direct translation.

00:24:28.790 --> 00:24:31.250
System A does it this way inside.

00:24:31.250 --> 00:24:36.940
Its most important thing is that the tail
has this form, or something like this.

00:24:36.940 --> 00:24:39.679
So there isn't this direct internal translation.

00:24:39.679 --> 00:24:45.720
Just as for humans, even if we could do brain-to-brain
transfer of thoughts, it's not really going

00:24:45.720 --> 00:24:46.940
to work.

00:24:46.940 --> 00:24:52.000
Just like when you have two machine-learning
systems, the details of how they learnt things

00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:53.580
inside will be different.

00:24:53.580 --> 00:24:58.559
And that thought experiment, so to speak,
about thoughts, of direct transfer of thoughts,

00:24:58.559 --> 00:25:00.000
that also applies.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:06.049
The thing that is the robust transfer of thoughts
is basically language.

00:25:06.049 --> 00:25:12.000
The thoughts themselves are not directly transferable,
but we package them into language, which is

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:18.340
this formal representation of thoughts that
we can transfer from one mind to another,

00:25:18.340 --> 00:25:19.340
so to speak.

00:25:19.340 --> 00:25:22.149
I think that's my way of thinking about that
at least.

00:25:22.149 --> 00:25:23.149
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:25:23.149 --> 00:25:26.460
This underscores, I think, part of the appeal
for me in learning these languages, even when

00:25:26.460 --> 00:25:28.080
they really have very little utility.

00:25:28.080 --> 00:25:32.460
I was just studying Romanian, which has very
limited use.

00:25:32.460 --> 00:25:41.830
A part of the fascination is, as you're mentioning,
these concepts or labels that take the form

00:25:41.830 --> 00:25:48.710
of language, even if the translations, literal
translation, can be conveyed to the target

00:25:48.710 --> 00:25:54.539
mind, the nuance can be much more challenging
to convey.

00:25:54.539 --> 00:26:01.090
And I find exploring the language is a way
to better understand the thinking of a target

00:26:01.090 --> 00:26:03.630
population, whether Japanese or Romanian,
to be a lot of fun.

00:26:03.630 --> 00:26:09.950
Because for instance in Japanese, there are
at least 40 or 50 ways to say "no."

00:26:09.950 --> 00:26:14.540
But they might take the form of, "Well, that's
very difficult," or, "Maybe that's possible.

00:26:14.540 --> 00:26:16.039
Let me ask Mr. Takahashi."

00:26:16.039 --> 00:26:19.179
And those all mean "no."

00:26:19.179 --> 00:26:22.290
But the translation won't necessarily convey
that.

00:26:22.290 --> 00:26:31.380
And also thinking about, and I'll stop my
mini TED Talk here in a second, but the structure

00:26:31.380 --> 00:26:35.860
of the language, let's just say something
as simple as subject verb object.

00:26:35.860 --> 00:26:41.460
"I eat the apple" versus "I, the apple eat,"
which you would find in Japanese and then

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:45.460
in German you would find it, but only in certain
cases where it's a relative clause.

00:26:45.460 --> 00:26:52.580
And all of that, I think, represents, often
represents fundamental differences in how

00:26:52.580 --> 00:26:55.240
people process reality.

00:26:55.240 --> 00:26:57.029
So I really enjoy it.

00:26:57.029 --> 00:27:03.820
Now, let me ask you a question about the type
of scientific forensic analysis that you've

00:27:03.820 --> 00:27:04.820
done.

00:27:04.820 --> 00:27:11.120
Where you're looking at how someone took a
left turn, in say, thermodynamics at some

00:27:11.120 --> 00:27:12.250
point in time.

00:27:12.250 --> 00:27:15.380
I have not read this book of yours, but Idea
Makers.

00:27:15.380 --> 00:27:16.669
It's a compilation of essays.

00:27:16.669 --> 00:27:20.179
How did you choose the players on the field
for this?

00:27:20.179 --> 00:27:22.529
How did you choose the people you included?

00:27:22.529 --> 00:27:26.290
Stephen Wolfram: Oh, it was always opportunistic.

00:27:26.290 --> 00:27:31.080
I'm afraid some of it was somebody died and
I knew them and I wanted to write an obituary

00:27:31.080 --> 00:27:32.669
post.

00:27:32.669 --> 00:27:38.240
Others were, somebody was having a big anniversary
and there was a big shindig associated with

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:40.769
that, so rather opportunistically.

00:27:40.769 --> 00:27:47.549
But it happened to cover a rather nice collection
of different types of folks from Dick Feynman

00:27:47.549 --> 00:27:52.750
and Steve Jobs, who were both people I knew,
to people like Ada Lovelace and Ramanujan,

00:27:52.750 --> 00:27:55.630
who are people who died long before I was
born.

00:27:55.630 --> 00:28:00.360
Tim Ferriss: So Dick Feynman, I'd love to,
if you would indulge me, just to tell me a

00:28:00.360 --> 00:28:03.960
bit of your experience with Dick Feynman.

00:28:03.960 --> 00:28:11.200
I own the set of Encyclopedia Britannica that
he bought when he was, I think, 43 years old.

00:28:11.200 --> 00:28:18.320
I ended up buying it on my 43rd birthday as
a reminder to never stop searching and learning.

00:28:18.320 --> 00:28:19.320
Stephen Wolfram: That's cool.

00:28:19.320 --> 00:28:25.610
Tim Ferriss: And I drew a few diagrams of
his as well, which I prize.

00:28:25.610 --> 00:28:28.330
And I wish I knew more physics.

00:28:28.330 --> 00:28:30.110
I really enjoyed it when I was younger.

00:28:30.110 --> 00:28:33.030
I did not pursue it to an advanced level at
all.

00:28:33.030 --> 00:28:37.470
I really wish I could appreciate his genius
in a higher fidelity way.

00:28:37.470 --> 00:28:40.970
But what was it like to spend time with him,
and how did you know him?

00:28:40.970 --> 00:28:42.840
What was he like in person?

00:28:42.840 --> 00:28:46.230
Stephen Wolfram: Yeah, I met him when I was
18 and he was 60.

00:28:46.230 --> 00:28:47.230
Okay?

00:28:47.230 --> 00:28:48.230
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:28:48.230 --> 00:28:53.080
Stephen Wolfram: And he would always say,
"I was as quick as you were, but now I'm three

00:28:53.080 --> 00:28:55.820
times older than you are."

00:28:55.820 --> 00:28:59.769
No, he could be quite competitive in those
kinds of things.

00:28:59.769 --> 00:29:09.519
But the thing that I liked about him, whenever
I see it, is he just would think about anything.

00:29:09.519 --> 00:29:15.860
It's like the thinking apparatus is engaged
and will stay engaged, whatever the topic,

00:29:15.860 --> 00:29:17.679
so to speak.

00:29:17.679 --> 00:29:22.580
And he liked drilling down to really get,
"What is the real point?

00:29:22.580 --> 00:29:25.390
What's the essence of what's going on?"

00:29:25.390 --> 00:29:30.179
And sometimes he would play a little trick
on the world, which was one of the things

00:29:30.179 --> 00:29:33.419
he was really good at was calculating things
by hand.

00:29:33.419 --> 00:29:40.130
He used computers a little bit, but mostly
hand calculation, complicated math, these

00:29:40.130 --> 00:29:41.471
kinds of things.

00:29:41.471 --> 00:29:45.080
And so he would do, he'd have some question,
he would do all this complicated math to work

00:29:45.080 --> 00:29:46.760
out the answer.

00:29:46.760 --> 00:29:49.789
Then he would get this answer, and then he
would think, "Well, how can I have figured

00:29:49.789 --> 00:29:54.669
out that answer by just some intuitive argument
without having to go through all the complicated

00:29:54.669 --> 00:29:55.669
math?"

00:29:55.669 --> 00:29:57.769
I remember talking to him about this.

00:29:57.769 --> 00:30:02.720
He thought that everybody can do the complicated
math, but it's really impressive if you can

00:30:02.720 --> 00:30:04.909
figure this out by intuition.

00:30:04.909 --> 00:30:09.309
And so he would then figure out this intuitive
answer, throw away the math, tell people only

00:30:09.309 --> 00:30:11.090
the intuition.

00:30:11.090 --> 00:30:14.490
There's some fields where he did that.

00:30:14.490 --> 00:30:18.070
And people are like, "We don't know how on
Earth he figured this out."

00:30:18.070 --> 00:30:25.020
And people have been trying to reverse engineer
the math for years, but that was one of his

00:30:25.020 --> 00:30:26.730
little clicks on the world, so to speak.

00:30:26.730 --> 00:30:28.890
Tim Ferriss: Oh, that's incredible.

00:30:28.890 --> 00:30:30.159
Intellectual sleight of hand.

00:30:30.159 --> 00:30:34.770
Would Ramanujan — this is someone I know
even less about.

00:30:34.770 --> 00:30:45.000
I personify the intuition that Feynman was
referring to, or on some level —

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:47.440
Stephen Wolfram: Well, Ramanujan is a different
story.

00:30:47.440 --> 00:30:57.399
Ramanujan was a slightly decently educated
person just hanging out in India and producing

00:30:57.399 --> 00:30:59.580
remarkable mathematical results.

00:30:59.580 --> 00:31:03.320
Ramanujan, like Feynman, was a very good calculator.

00:31:03.320 --> 00:31:07.900
And it really confused the mathematicians
because he would say, "I've got this amazing

00:31:07.900 --> 00:31:08.919
formula for Pi.

00:31:08.919 --> 00:31:11.370
Nobody's ever seen anything like it."

00:31:11.370 --> 00:31:13.799
And he'd say, "Here it is."

00:31:13.799 --> 00:31:18.720
And then when he started corresponding with
mathematicians in England in 1913, or whatever,

00:31:18.720 --> 00:31:20.360
they were like, "Well, how did you prove this?

00:31:20.360 --> 00:31:22.110
How do you know this?"

00:31:22.110 --> 00:31:26.539
And eventually he got so fed up, I think,
that he told them, "Well, the goddess so and

00:31:26.539 --> 00:31:29.380
so told it to me in a dream."

00:31:29.380 --> 00:31:32.490
But the truth of it was, he was a really good
calculator.

00:31:32.490 --> 00:31:37.659
And so he just worked out, this particular
series is the same as Pi to this number of

00:31:37.659 --> 00:31:39.019
decimal places.

00:31:39.019 --> 00:31:43.210
And he had good enough mathematical intuition
to say, "And it's just going to be correct.

00:31:43.210 --> 00:31:46.750
It's not just going to be an approximation,
it's going to be exactly right."

00:31:46.750 --> 00:31:48.471
Occasionally, that intuition failed him.

00:31:48.471 --> 00:31:55.429
He had a result about prime numbers, for example,
where he had done many, many, many cases and

00:31:55.429 --> 00:31:57.159
it looked like it was true.

00:31:57.159 --> 00:31:59.710
Turns out the result isn't true.

00:31:59.710 --> 00:32:05.560
But the first exception is that 10 to the
10 to the hundred or something, some huge

00:32:05.560 --> 00:32:09.360
place which he couldn't reach with calculation.

00:32:09.360 --> 00:32:14.070
So in a sense he was, I think, a great experimental
mathematician.

00:32:14.070 --> 00:32:19.870
Had he used computers, he would've had a whole
different set of things he could've discovered.

00:32:19.870 --> 00:32:23.470
But even with himself as the computer, so
to speak, he was discovering all kinds of

00:32:23.470 --> 00:32:24.470
things.

00:32:24.470 --> 00:32:28.559
And literally, the mathematicians of the time
had never seen anything like it.

00:32:28.559 --> 00:32:34.179
And so for them, it just seemed to be this
magical thing where he was just pulling formulas

00:32:34.179 --> 00:32:35.380
from nothing.

00:32:35.380 --> 00:32:36.929
A bit like Dick Feynman.

00:32:36.929 --> 00:32:41.260
He was often pulling formulas from a lot of
hard work of computation.

00:32:41.260 --> 00:32:48.340
And I remember when I was younger, I happened
to start using computers to do physics very

00:32:48.340 --> 00:32:51.470
early on when I was a teenager, and so on.

00:32:51.470 --> 00:32:56.909
And for some reason the tools existed, but
to be able to do some mathematical computations

00:32:56.909 --> 00:32:59.790
by computer, but people weren't using them.

00:32:59.790 --> 00:33:08.340
One of the experiences that I had when I was
a kid was I discovered the fact that computers

00:33:08.340 --> 00:33:15.320
can be really powerful for doing science and
so on when I was 13, 14, 15 years old.

00:33:15.320 --> 00:33:20.649
And I started using them to do that, and I
started being able to derive all kinds of

00:33:20.649 --> 00:33:24.100
complicated mathematical formulas and so on
by computer.

00:33:24.100 --> 00:33:27.950
For whatever crazy reason, other people just
weren't doing that.

00:33:27.950 --> 00:33:32.659
And so I could write physics papers where
I would have these very elaborate formulas

00:33:32.659 --> 00:33:35.029
that I derived, and so on.

00:33:35.029 --> 00:33:39.730
And people were like, "You must be amazingly
good at doing all this calculational stuff."

00:33:39.730 --> 00:33:41.170
And it's like, well no, actually.

00:33:41.170 --> 00:33:46.970
I'm actually pretty bad at it, but me with
a computer we’re pretty good.

00:33:46.970 --> 00:33:51.350
But they didn't really even understand the
fact that that was a thing back in those days.

00:33:51.350 --> 00:34:00.059
Yeah, I've seen this interesting phenomenon
of when you use tools where people don't necessarily

00:34:00.059 --> 00:34:04.190
even understand that those tools exist it
has some interesting consequences.

00:34:04.190 --> 00:34:05.250
I find, for me —
Tim Ferriss: I was just going to say, the

00:34:05.250 --> 00:34:07.070
attribution, I'm going to skip.

00:34:07.070 --> 00:34:11.470
But what was it, any sufficiently advanced
form of technologies indistinguishable from

00:34:11.470 --> 00:34:12.470
magic?

00:34:12.470 --> 00:34:13.470
Something like that.

00:34:13.470 --> 00:34:14.470
I can't remember what the attribution would
be.

00:34:14.470 --> 00:34:15.470
Stephen Wolfram: — Arthur C. Clarke.

00:34:15.470 --> 00:34:16.560
Tim Ferriss: I think that sounds like Arthur
C. Clarke.

00:34:16.560 --> 00:34:17.560
Stephen Wolfram: Right.

00:34:17.560 --> 00:34:18.560
Yeah.

00:34:18.560 --> 00:34:24.560
No, I think that the thing that I've spent
a lot of my scientific effort getting intuition

00:34:24.560 --> 00:34:26.970
from doing computer experiments.

00:34:26.970 --> 00:34:32.119
In another age I would've been there with
test tubes and other things doing physical

00:34:32.119 --> 00:34:33.119
experiments.

00:34:33.119 --> 00:34:37.320
Thank goodness I can get away with doing computer
experiments, because it's a lot better for

00:34:37.320 --> 00:34:38.899
me, so to speak.

00:34:38.899 --> 00:34:44.760
But you do these experiments and there's a
certain art to doing a good computer experiment,

00:34:44.760 --> 00:34:50.530
but you can discover things that you never
thought were there, and they inform your intuition

00:34:50.530 --> 00:34:52.300
and allow you to build things up.

00:34:52.300 --> 00:34:54.290
And it's this thing that comes from nowhere.

00:34:54.290 --> 00:34:59.000
Because it's just coming from not the natural
world, but the computational world.

00:34:59.000 --> 00:35:04.030
You're just turning over this rock in the
computational world and suddenly you discover

00:35:04.030 --> 00:35:07.970
that there's this whole crazy thing going
on underneath it, so to speak.

00:35:07.970 --> 00:35:13.830
Tim Ferriss: I would love to pose a question
that was posed at a group dinner I attended

00:35:13.830 --> 00:35:20.190
not too long ago, and it's related to what
the moderator called heresies.

00:35:20.190 --> 00:35:21.190
And I'll unpack what that means.

00:35:21.190 --> 00:35:22.190
He asked each of us to present a heresy.

00:35:22.190 --> 00:35:24.339
And the heresy in this context was something
that the people in this small group, about

00:35:24.339 --> 00:35:26.210
six to eight people, would disagree with you
about.

00:35:26.210 --> 00:35:29.680
And these were a lot of technical folks.

00:35:29.680 --> 00:35:34.040
And I think each person in the group could
have put out many things they believed that

00:35:34.040 --> 00:35:36.810
the broader population would disagree with
them about.

00:35:36.810 --> 00:35:46.241
But are there any particular beliefs that
you have or scientific insights, computational

00:35:46.241 --> 00:35:52.530
insights, this could be related to the Physics
Project, could be related to other things?

00:35:52.530 --> 00:35:57.589
Things you believe that you have high conviction
in that many people would disagree with you

00:35:57.589 --> 00:36:03.150
on now, but let's just say hopefully 10 years
from now looking back they would say, "Ah,

00:36:03.150 --> 00:36:06.040
yes, that was actually, in fact, had some
grounds to it."

00:36:06.040 --> 00:36:07.730
Does anything come to mind?

00:36:07.730 --> 00:36:11.790
Stephen Wolfram: Yeah, there's several, but
there's one example of a big one.

00:36:11.790 --> 00:36:20.890
But this one, the resistance is crumbling
rapidly, maybe it doesn't quite count as well.

00:36:20.890 --> 00:36:28.589
But I think it would still be the case that
if you poll, let's say physicists, the resistance

00:36:28.589 --> 00:36:30.980
would not have completely crumbled.

00:36:30.980 --> 00:36:34.890
And that's the question of, "What is space?

00:36:34.890 --> 00:36:38.230
And is space made of anything?"

00:36:38.230 --> 00:36:42.060
So go back, let's say 120 years.

00:36:42.060 --> 00:36:44.480
People said, "What's water made of?"

00:36:44.480 --> 00:36:46.150
Water isn't made of anything.

00:36:46.150 --> 00:36:50.630
Water is just a fluid that flows in the way
it flows.

00:36:50.630 --> 00:36:55.740
Turns out what became clear than the late
19th century, is actually water is made of

00:36:55.740 --> 00:36:56.740
something.

00:36:56.740 --> 00:36:58.990
People have guessed it much earlier, it's
made of molecules.

00:36:58.990 --> 00:37:01.510
So the question now is, "What is space made
of?

00:37:01.510 --> 00:37:10.230
This thing that we move around in, is it just
a thing where we get to place something wherever

00:37:10.230 --> 00:37:11.300
we want?

00:37:11.300 --> 00:37:12.300
Or does it have an inner structure?"

00:37:12.300 --> 00:37:17.460
And I've been pretty sure for quite a while
that space has an inner structure, it's just

00:37:17.460 --> 00:37:22.670
made of these discrete atoms of existence.

00:37:22.670 --> 00:37:25.710
You can think of in the case of space, atoms
of space.

00:37:25.710 --> 00:37:30.010
And all that one can say is how these atoms
of space are connected to other ones.

00:37:30.010 --> 00:37:35.390
So there's this giant network that defines
the structure of space, and that's what space

00:37:35.390 --> 00:37:37.040
ultimately is.

00:37:37.040 --> 00:37:41.920
And everything that exists in the universe
is a feature of the way those connections

00:37:41.920 --> 00:37:44.250
work in the underlying structure of space.

00:37:44.250 --> 00:37:48.460
That's kind of like, if you have a fluid,
there's a bunch of these molecules bouncing

00:37:48.460 --> 00:37:49.460
around.

00:37:49.460 --> 00:37:53.660
But let's say you just look at little vortex,
little eddy on the surface of the fluid, little

00:37:53.660 --> 00:37:56.370
whirlpool type thing.

00:37:56.370 --> 00:37:58.930
We can say, there's a whirlpool, you can see
it go by.

00:37:58.930 --> 00:38:01.240
And we can talk about it and so on.

00:38:01.240 --> 00:38:04.810
But ultimately, it's just made of a bunch
of molecules moving in a particular organized

00:38:04.810 --> 00:38:06.300
fashion.

00:38:06.300 --> 00:38:11.820
And it's my strong belief that everything
we know in the universe, all the electrons

00:38:11.820 --> 00:38:17.440
and photons, and the things we that get made
up of those, they're all just things like

00:38:17.440 --> 00:38:23.640
eddies in the structure of this giant network
that is the underlying data structure of the

00:38:23.640 --> 00:38:26.980
universe, and the underlying thing that space
is made of.

00:38:26.980 --> 00:38:29.420
So that's something —
Tim Ferriss: Where would dark matter fit into

00:38:29.420 --> 00:38:33.280
this, be vetoed by this, be compatible or
incompatible with this?

00:38:33.280 --> 00:38:36.010
Stephen Wolfram: It's kind of a detail.

00:38:36.010 --> 00:38:44.270
The bigger picture is something which is a
more embarrassing feature of current physics.

00:38:44.270 --> 00:38:49.710
Dark matter is the rotations of galaxies look
like there's more stuff inside the galaxy

00:38:49.710 --> 00:38:53.990
than you can account for by looking at the
luminous stars and so on.

00:38:53.990 --> 00:38:59.300
But there's a bigger embarrassment, which
is that in quantum field theory, the standard

00:38:59.300 --> 00:39:05.369
theory of the way small scale stuff happens
in physics, there is this phenomenon called

00:39:05.369 --> 00:39:07.319
zero point fluctuations.

00:39:07.319 --> 00:39:11.560
And there are an infinite collection of zero
point fluctuations in the universe that essentially

00:39:11.560 --> 00:39:15.240
produce energy, or are associated with energy
that has a gravitational effect that will

00:39:15.240 --> 00:39:19.780
roll the universe up into a tiny ball.

00:39:19.780 --> 00:39:21.200
That is not what we see.

00:39:21.200 --> 00:39:25.260
We see a universe that's a big universe, not
a universe that's just rolled up into a tiny

00:39:25.260 --> 00:39:26.260
ball.

00:39:26.260 --> 00:39:33.640
And so that's, in terms of missing energy,
that is a much bigger by a factor of a hundred

00:39:33.640 --> 00:39:35.240
orders of magnitude or more.

00:39:35.240 --> 00:39:37.560
That's a bigger problem, so to speak, than
the dark energy problem.

00:39:37.560 --> 00:39:38.560
But that's a dark matter problem.

00:39:38.560 --> 00:39:39.560
Dark energy is yet a different problem.

00:39:39.560 --> 00:39:44.780
But these are all features of, there's aspects
of the universe where there's energy, but

00:39:44.780 --> 00:39:48.240
where the energy doesn't seem to have the
effect that we would expect to have, or we

00:39:48.240 --> 00:39:50.819
don't know where the energy is coming from,
and so on.

00:39:50.819 --> 00:39:58.430
One feature of our of model of physics is
that the very processes that are leading to

00:39:58.430 --> 00:40:03.119
those vacuum fluctuations and so on, those
are the processes that knit together the structure

00:40:03.119 --> 00:40:04.370
of space.

00:40:04.370 --> 00:40:08.500
So in the usual theory, it's like there's
space and that's a thing.

00:40:08.500 --> 00:40:13.270
And then there's all this matter that is doing
all these weird quantum fluctuations in space,

00:40:13.270 --> 00:40:16.300
and that matter should have more of an effect
on space.

00:40:16.300 --> 00:40:22.530
But in our models, those quantum fluctuation-like
things, those are what make space.

00:40:22.530 --> 00:40:28.869
And so it's not surprising, and the math works
out this way, that the thing that makes space

00:40:28.869 --> 00:40:33.092
doesn't itself have the effect on space of
doing something like curling the universe

00:40:33.092 --> 00:40:35.420
up into a tiny ball.

00:40:35.420 --> 00:40:39.630
That's an example of how something like that
works out in our model of physics.

00:40:39.630 --> 00:40:44.680
Tim Ferriss: I do have a question about time,
which I'll get to in a second.

00:40:44.680 --> 00:40:49.450
But any other heresies that would be on the
short list of things that come to mind?

00:40:49.450 --> 00:40:50.450
Stephen Wolfram: Oh, boy.

00:40:50.450 --> 00:40:52.750
Here's another one, it's a little bit more
detailed.

00:40:52.750 --> 00:40:59.770
See, what's happened is, as a result of the
Physics Project we have come to understand

00:40:59.770 --> 00:41:04.619
a different paradigmatic way of thinking about
a bunch of things.

00:41:04.619 --> 00:41:08.940
And so there are a bunch of fields that you
can then apply this new paradigmatic way of

00:41:08.940 --> 00:41:15.640
thinking to, and start to make foundational
changes in those fields.

00:41:15.640 --> 00:41:18.460
I'll mention another thing, I don't know how
much of a heresy this is.

00:41:18.460 --> 00:41:24.390
But in physics, the two big theories of physics
— well, there are really three big theories

00:41:24.390 --> 00:41:31.430
of 20th century physics: general relativity,
Einstein's theory of gravity, quantum mechanics,

00:41:31.430 --> 00:41:33.350
and statistical mechanics.

00:41:33.350 --> 00:41:37.460
Which is what brings us things like the second
law of thermodynamics, the law of entropy

00:41:37.460 --> 00:41:38.589
increase, and so on.

00:41:38.589 --> 00:41:41.390
And it's the theory of heat, so to speak.

00:41:41.390 --> 00:41:44.910
Those are the three big theories of 20th century
physics.

00:41:44.910 --> 00:41:50.350
One of the things that I think is just super
amazingly cool, is it's turned out that all

00:41:50.350 --> 00:41:53.349
three of those theories are basically come
from the same place.

00:41:53.349 --> 00:41:56.359
They're all in a sense versions of the same
statement.

00:41:56.359 --> 00:41:58.740
They're all in some sense the same theory.

00:41:58.740 --> 00:42:01.609
Which, to me, is really remarkable.

00:42:01.609 --> 00:42:06.920
And in fact, one of the things that has just
become clear to me now is that the people

00:42:06.920 --> 00:42:13.060
have thought — well, okay, so statistical
mechanics is about when you put lots of molecules

00:42:13.060 --> 00:42:15.730
together, what do they typically do?

00:42:15.730 --> 00:42:20.170
For example, you can have molecules that make
a gas, and you say there are certain gas laws

00:42:20.170 --> 00:42:23.589
that determine the pressure and volume of
the gas, and so on.

00:42:23.589 --> 00:42:28.130
These are typical things that happen when
you just throw a bunch of molecules altogether.

00:42:28.130 --> 00:42:30.670
That's what statistical mechanics is about.

00:42:30.670 --> 00:42:37.131
And people have believed that the most significant
thing in statistical mechanics, the second

00:42:37.131 --> 00:42:42.780
law of thermodynamics, which is the law that
says things tend to get more random.

00:42:42.780 --> 00:42:48.339
When you have mechanical work that's doing
things, eventually that's dissipated as heat.

00:42:48.339 --> 00:42:53.100
And once it's heat, that's microscopic motion
of molecules, you never get back that large

00:42:53.100 --> 00:42:54.380
scale mechanical motion.

00:42:54.380 --> 00:43:00.700
It's turned into heat, it's random, you don't
get anything back from it.

00:43:00.700 --> 00:43:05.680
People have believed that it's possible to
derive the second law of thermodynamics in

00:43:05.680 --> 00:43:07.599
some kind of almost mathematical way.

00:43:07.599 --> 00:43:13.520
You don't really need to know physics to be
able to derive that principle that things

00:43:13.520 --> 00:43:17.000
tend to, for example, go from mechanical work
to heat.

00:43:17.000 --> 00:43:22.839
But people have believed that general relativity
and quantum mechanics are both wheel-in features

00:43:22.839 --> 00:43:23.930
of our universe.

00:43:23.930 --> 00:43:28.280
They're both things where you could have made
a universe that didn't have one of those things,

00:43:28.280 --> 00:43:31.569
they're just things where it happened to be
that way.

00:43:31.569 --> 00:43:36.109
What has become clear from our Physics Project
is that all three of these theories of come

00:43:36.109 --> 00:43:39.940
from the same place, and they're all as derivable
with each other.

00:43:39.940 --> 00:43:45.480
And they're all derivable in a really interesting
way.

00:43:45.480 --> 00:43:49.690
They're all derivable ultimately from this
strange thing we call the ruliad, which is

00:43:49.690 --> 00:43:52.530
this limit of all possible computations.

00:43:52.530 --> 00:43:56.890
The entangled limit of all possible computations.

00:43:56.890 --> 00:44:01.440
And what turns out to happen is all three
of those theories are the results of observers

00:44:01.440 --> 00:44:05.950
like us sampling this ruliad object.

00:44:05.950 --> 00:44:10.860
And what matters is that we have certain attributes
as observers.

00:44:10.860 --> 00:44:13.340
For example, we are computationally bounded.

00:44:13.340 --> 00:44:18.440
We can only fit a limited amount of computational
stuff into our minds.

00:44:18.440 --> 00:44:22.470
We can't describe, oh, here's where every
atom in the universe went.

00:44:22.470 --> 00:44:28.510
In our minds, the narrative that we have for
describing the universe is far away from,

00:44:28.510 --> 00:44:30.420
let's describe where every atom went.

00:44:30.420 --> 00:44:36.220
We're just talking about these much more filtered
versions of what's going on in the universe.

00:44:36.220 --> 00:44:38.380
That turns out to be one of the important
things.

00:44:38.380 --> 00:44:41.930
The other one is that we believe we are persistent
in time.

00:44:41.930 --> 00:44:47.490
In other words, even though at every moment
we are made from different atoms of space,

00:44:47.490 --> 00:44:51.680
we are being the atoms of space that we were
at one moment are being destroyed, new ones

00:44:51.680 --> 00:44:53.780
are being created and so on.

00:44:53.780 --> 00:44:57.020
Despite that, we believe that we are persistent
in time.

00:44:57.020 --> 00:44:59.630
It's like the little eddy on the water.

00:44:59.630 --> 00:45:03.630
The molecules that make that eddy, it's different
molecules at every moment in time.

00:45:03.630 --> 00:45:09.530
Yet there is a definite thing, which if the
eddy had a mind, so to speak, it could think

00:45:09.530 --> 00:45:12.170
it is persistent in time.

00:45:12.170 --> 00:45:17.870
Those two features that our minds are computationally
bounded and we believe we are persistent in

00:45:17.870 --> 00:45:24.680
time, those two features determine how we
sample this ruliad thing, which is the ultimate

00:45:24.680 --> 00:45:27.640
limit of all possible processes.

00:45:27.640 --> 00:45:32.359
And the sampling that we get to do is one
that gives us those three features, those

00:45:32.359 --> 00:45:34.050
three big theories of 20th century physics.

00:45:34.050 --> 00:45:35.950
Tim Ferriss: How do you spell ruliad?

00:45:35.950 --> 00:45:37.730
Stephen Wolfram: R-U-L-I-A-D.

00:45:37.730 --> 00:45:39.980
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I got it.

00:45:39.980 --> 00:45:40.980
Look at that.

00:45:40.980 --> 00:45:41.980
Incredible.

00:45:41.980 --> 00:45:50.089
Now, is there a lay explanation or exploration
of the ruliad that isn't completely corrupted?

00:45:50.089 --> 00:45:51.089
That someone like —
Stephen Wolfram: Yeah, I think so.

00:45:51.089 --> 00:45:52.089
Tim Ferriss: — myself might digest?

00:45:52.089 --> 00:45:57.910
Stephen Wolfram: The first thing to think
about is, there's a little bit of a story

00:45:57.910 --> 00:45:59.650
that gets to it.

00:45:59.650 --> 00:46:05.690
One of the things I've long been interested
in is, is there a simple rule from which where

00:46:05.690 --> 00:46:10.200
you just run that rule long enough and you'll
get everything that happens in the universe?

00:46:10.200 --> 00:46:14.339
In other words, I was talking before about
how simple computational rules you run them,

00:46:14.339 --> 00:46:16.210
they do really complicated things.

00:46:16.210 --> 00:46:20.680
The most complicated thing we know about is
the whole universe, so could we find a rule?

00:46:20.680 --> 00:46:25.290
Or we just write down this rule and we could
run it long enough, it would just make the

00:46:25.290 --> 00:46:26.670
whole universe.

00:46:26.670 --> 00:46:34.380
Okay, then you start thinking, well, let's
imagine that we had that rule.

00:46:34.380 --> 00:46:39.900
Let's imagine that we'd found that — let
me go actually one other place first, which

00:46:39.900 --> 00:46:43.690
is how quantum mechanics works.

00:46:43.690 --> 00:46:51.809
In classical mechanics you have laws that
describe how things move, or what happens

00:46:51.809 --> 00:46:53.300
in the world.

00:46:53.300 --> 00:46:58.390
There might be something that says, I throw
a ball with a certain velocity, it will move

00:46:58.390 --> 00:46:59.690
in a certain trajectory.

00:46:59.690 --> 00:47:03.599
Tim Ferriss: And by classical, you mean Newtonian
in this case?

00:47:03.599 --> 00:47:04.650
Stephen Wolfram: Newtonian physics, yes.

00:47:04.650 --> 00:47:05.650
Tim Ferriss: Got it.

00:47:05.650 --> 00:47:08.550
Stephen Wolfram: Actually, the relativistic
physics works the same way.

00:47:08.550 --> 00:47:15.720
That's the distinction between classical as
in not quantum and quantum.

00:47:15.720 --> 00:47:19.570
The dividing line is around 1920, it's about
100 years old, the thing.

00:47:19.570 --> 00:47:24.930
And so, in the quantum view of the world,
it isn't the case that definite things happen.

00:47:24.930 --> 00:47:28.470
Instead, the quantum view is there are many
paths that get followed.

00:47:28.470 --> 00:47:34.250
That was Dick Feynman’s idea, this idea
of path integrals and following many quantum

00:47:34.250 --> 00:47:35.640
paths.

00:47:35.640 --> 00:47:41.770
But the notion is that in quantum mechanics
lots of different things happen.

00:47:41.770 --> 00:47:44.180
The ball goes on many different possible trajectories.

00:47:44.180 --> 00:47:50.670
We, as observers of what happened, we get
to sample across those possibilities and just

00:47:50.670 --> 00:47:53.940
get to say, "Oh, there was a certain probability
that this would happen.

00:47:53.940 --> 00:47:56.369
There's a certain probability that would happen."

00:47:56.369 --> 00:48:00.540
That's the traditional view of quantum mechanics.

00:48:00.540 --> 00:48:06.710
In our models you have this giant graph that
represents this giant network, that represents

00:48:06.710 --> 00:48:08.380
the structure of the universe.

00:48:08.380 --> 00:48:12.990
And it's continually being rewritten according
to some rule.

00:48:12.990 --> 00:48:17.650
What turns out to happen is, there are many
different possible rewrites that could occur.

00:48:17.650 --> 00:48:21.540
Those different possible rewrites give you
these different paths of history.

00:48:21.540 --> 00:48:27.000
They give you essentially different threads
of time, so to speak.

00:48:27.000 --> 00:48:29.589
Different possible things that could happen
in the universe.

00:48:29.589 --> 00:48:34.599
Those threads of history, sometimes they branch,
because two different things could happen

00:48:34.599 --> 00:48:35.599
next.

00:48:35.599 --> 00:48:40.370
Sometimes they merge, because two things end
up producing essentially the same universe.

00:48:40.370 --> 00:48:45.560
So you end up with this whole complicated
structure of branching and merging of possible

00:48:45.560 --> 00:48:46.740
histories for the universe.

00:48:46.740 --> 00:48:52.060
So now the question is, how do we perceive
what's going on in that universe?

00:48:52.060 --> 00:48:56.730
And why do we not see the universe as this
thing where it's branching all over the place?

00:48:56.730 --> 00:48:58.330
And how can we tell what's happening?

00:48:58.330 --> 00:49:02.849
Well, the thing we have to realize is that
we ourselves are embedded in this branching

00:49:02.849 --> 00:49:04.020
universe.

00:49:04.020 --> 00:49:08.940
So our minds are branching just like everything
else in the universe is branching.

00:49:08.940 --> 00:49:14.730
It turns out the core question of how one
perceives quantum mechanics is, how does a

00:49:14.730 --> 00:49:19.109
branching mind perceive a branching universe?

00:49:19.109 --> 00:49:23.520
And so then this thing that I mentioned that's
a feature of us, is we believe that we are

00:49:23.520 --> 00:49:25.430
persistent in time.

00:49:25.430 --> 00:49:32.180
And so even though in some sort of external
God's eye view, so to speak, the universe

00:49:32.180 --> 00:49:39.460
is branching like crazy, we believe that our
minds are going through a single thread of

00:49:39.460 --> 00:49:40.940
experience.

00:49:40.940 --> 00:49:46.590
And so that means as we impose that belief
on what's actually going on in the universe,

00:49:46.590 --> 00:49:52.010
we conflate lots of different paths that from
the outside would look like the universe is

00:49:52.010 --> 00:49:53.010
doing different things.

00:49:53.010 --> 00:49:57.910
But we so know, actually, those are all in
some sense the same thing, because that's

00:49:57.910 --> 00:50:02.830
what we have to believe in order to have this
conceit that we have a definite thread of

00:50:02.830 --> 00:50:04.200
experience.

00:50:04.200 --> 00:50:10.240
And so that process is what drives the understanding
of how quantum mechanics works.

00:50:10.240 --> 00:50:12.490
And actually, returning to Dick Feynman again.

00:50:12.490 --> 00:50:17.589
He always used to say that having worked his
whole life on quantum mechanics, he always

00:50:17.589 --> 00:50:21.420
was very fond of saying nobody understands
quantum mechanics.

00:50:21.420 --> 00:50:24.670
And I talked to him for ages and ages about
that.

00:50:24.670 --> 00:50:29.680
And I wish he was still around because I think
I can finally say I think I actually understand

00:50:29.680 --> 00:50:30.940
quantum mechanics.

00:50:30.940 --> 00:50:36.800
And it's just this idea of the branching mind
perceiving the branching universe.

00:50:36.800 --> 00:50:38.640
I hadn't seen that coming at all.

00:50:38.640 --> 00:50:45.040
And it's a bizarre idea that turns out, I
think, to unlock how that works.

00:50:45.040 --> 00:50:49.570
But okay, in quantum mechanics we have all
these different possible things that could

00:50:49.570 --> 00:50:55.540
happen in the universe, which to us get conflated
together into a definite path.

00:50:55.540 --> 00:51:02.730
Well, okay, let's say we've got this model
and we say we find this rule, and this rule

00:51:02.730 --> 00:51:05.049
represents everything the universe does.

00:51:05.049 --> 00:51:09.520
So then we might imagine this day where we've
got this rule comes out of our computer, we've

00:51:09.520 --> 00:51:15.260
done some search and we have rule number 713
is our universe.

00:51:15.260 --> 00:51:22.130
For a long time I was just really uncomfortable
with that idea.

00:51:22.130 --> 00:51:28.730
Because let's say we're universe 713, the
next question is, why did we get number 713?

00:51:28.730 --> 00:51:32.809
Why didn't we get number seven trillion, whatever?

00:51:32.809 --> 00:51:34.760
Why this one?

00:51:34.760 --> 00:51:39.560
One of the big lessons of science over the
last 500 years is the Copernicus lesson.

00:51:39.560 --> 00:51:41.300
We're not very special.

00:51:41.300 --> 00:51:43.910
We might have thought the Earth was the center
of the universe.

00:51:43.910 --> 00:51:46.540
We might have thought these kinds of things,
but it isn't true.

00:51:46.540 --> 00:51:54.380
We're just on a random planet somewhere in
this random space that makes up the universe.

00:51:54.380 --> 00:51:59.300
Even the idea that our rule is a simple rule,
as opposed to an incredibly complicated rule,

00:51:59.300 --> 00:52:01.869
seems very anti-Copernican.

00:52:01.869 --> 00:52:03.329
This really bothered me for a long time.

00:52:03.329 --> 00:52:08.490
And I realized, actually, something even more
bizarre may be going on.

00:52:08.490 --> 00:52:13.910
Which is, maybe the universe is not picking
any particular rule, it's running all possible

00:52:13.910 --> 00:52:15.150
rules.

00:52:15.150 --> 00:52:21.319
And so what the ruliad is is this computational
process that runs all possible rules.

00:52:21.319 --> 00:52:26.680
So imagine you had all possible computers
and you start them off from all possible starting

00:52:26.680 --> 00:52:28.660
points, and you run them all.

00:52:28.660 --> 00:52:31.609
You might say, "How could that do anything
interesting?"

00:52:31.609 --> 00:52:37.870
The critical point is that sometimes those
computers will end up making the same thing.

00:52:37.870 --> 00:52:43.400
In other words, two different computers might
end up having being producing something which

00:52:43.400 --> 00:52:45.940
is the same, has the same structure.

00:52:45.940 --> 00:52:49.230
And so you might say, "Well, they're just
all going to do their independent things."

00:52:49.230 --> 00:52:53.140
Well, they don't do independent things because
there are all these equivalences between things

00:52:53.140 --> 00:52:54.140
that they do.

00:52:54.140 --> 00:52:58.090
And so you end up building up this rich structure,
and that structure you build up is what we

00:52:58.090 --> 00:52:59.480
call the ruliad.

00:52:59.480 --> 00:53:03.420
It's the entangled limit of all possible computations.

00:53:03.420 --> 00:53:10.329
And what's really interesting about it is,
there's only one of it, and it is a necessary

00:53:10.329 --> 00:53:11.329
object.

00:53:11.329 --> 00:53:16.619
In other words, as soon as you define that
you're talking about the notion of computation.

00:53:16.619 --> 00:53:21.390
As soon as you define your terms, you have
the ruliad.

00:53:21.390 --> 00:53:26.890
It's not the case that it's like, oh, it so
happens that this feature of the world is

00:53:26.890 --> 00:53:27.970
this way.

00:53:27.970 --> 00:53:34.100
It is as inevitable as, once you define what
integers are and what plus signs are, and

00:53:34.100 --> 00:53:35.100
so on.

00:53:35.100 --> 00:53:36.240
Two plus two equals four.

00:53:36.240 --> 00:53:38.049
There's no way of getting out of that.

00:53:38.049 --> 00:53:45.450
It's not something that is a random fact about
the world that humans happen to have two eyes

00:53:45.450 --> 00:53:50.670
and a nose type thing, which might be seen
to be more coincidental.

00:53:50.670 --> 00:53:55.510
It's something that is a necessary feature
from the formal structure of what you've set

00:53:55.510 --> 00:53:56.510
up.

00:53:56.510 --> 00:53:58.619
So the ruliad is this necessary object.

00:53:58.619 --> 00:54:04.700
And now the thing which is interesting is,
okay, so you have this object that is this

00:54:04.700 --> 00:54:06.950
limitable possible computations.

00:54:06.950 --> 00:54:09.020
How do we experience that?

00:54:09.020 --> 00:54:12.250
Well, we are also part of that object.

00:54:12.250 --> 00:54:17.470
So it's the same story as the branching mind
perceiving the branching universe, except

00:54:17.470 --> 00:54:20.420
an even more abstracted version of that.

00:54:20.420 --> 00:54:28.990
It's, how do we as elements inside this ruliad
perceive the whole ruliad?

00:54:28.990 --> 00:54:34.240
One of the things one starts talking about
is this notion of what we call rulial space,

00:54:34.240 --> 00:54:39.069
which is the space of kind of possible different
views of how the universe works.

00:54:39.069 --> 00:54:44.369
So we might say we've got one view of the
universe, "Oh, it works this way, it follows

00:54:44.369 --> 00:54:45.440
this rule."

00:54:45.440 --> 00:54:49.850
And then some other person, alien, whatever,
says, "No, no, no, you're quite wrong.

00:54:49.850 --> 00:54:53.220
The universe really works according to this
other rule instead."

00:54:53.220 --> 00:54:58.819
Now the reason, what knits all of that together,
is a kind of technical fact that's been known

00:54:58.819 --> 00:55:02.960
for about a hundred years, which is this idea
of universal computation.

00:55:02.960 --> 00:55:08.300
You might have thought that if you wanted
to have a computer that was a word processor,

00:55:08.300 --> 00:55:09.480
you'd buy a word processing computer.

00:55:09.480 --> 00:55:13.000
You want to have a spreadsheet computer, you'd
buy a spreadsheet computer.

00:55:13.000 --> 00:55:18.260
But the big fact that emerged in the 1920s
and 1930s is that you can have a single sort

00:55:18.260 --> 00:55:19.329
of hardware object.

00:55:19.329 --> 00:55:24.050
I mean, it wasn't put to practice until the
1940s and '50s, but it was, you can have the

00:55:24.050 --> 00:55:30.140
single hardware object that can just be programmed
to be a word processor, be a spreadsheet,

00:55:30.140 --> 00:55:31.140
whatever.

00:55:31.140 --> 00:55:35.280
And it's kind of the same thing with the universe,
that you can attribute different rules to

00:55:35.280 --> 00:55:39.680
the operation of the universe, but they're
convertible, in the same way as your computer

00:55:39.680 --> 00:55:44.450
can be made to run a spreadsheet rather than
a word processor, so to speak.

00:55:44.450 --> 00:55:45.450
So —
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, finish your thought and

00:55:45.450 --> 00:55:46.450
then I want to pause for a second, ask a follow-up.

00:55:46.450 --> 00:55:48.200
Stephen Wolfram: So one of the things that
I really kind of like about this is there's

00:55:48.200 --> 00:55:50.880
this notion of where are you in rulial space?

00:55:50.880 --> 00:55:55.640
Where what kind of mode of description of
what's going on in the world do you have?

00:55:55.640 --> 00:56:01.770
And you can imagine that every different mind
is at a different place in rulial space.

00:56:01.770 --> 00:56:09.670
So the fact that you and I have different
internal models of the world is a statement

00:56:09.670 --> 00:56:13.210
of the fact that we are some distance apart
in rulial space.

00:56:13.210 --> 00:56:18.390
And so what you realize is, as we think about
the universe, we have the exploration of the

00:56:18.390 --> 00:56:22.039
universe by spacecraft or whatever going out
in physical space.

00:56:22.039 --> 00:56:25.480
We also have the exploration of the universe
in rulial space.

00:56:25.480 --> 00:56:30.490
And that's kind of, the different minds and
different ways of describing the universe

00:56:30.490 --> 00:56:33.280
represent kind of travel through rulial space.

00:56:33.280 --> 00:56:38.690
And just as we kind of, when we send out spacecraft
in physical space, we're exploring different

00:56:38.690 --> 00:56:43.869
parts of the physical universe, when we come
up with different ways of thinking about things

00:56:43.869 --> 00:56:46.890
and different ideas, we're kind of traveling
in rulial space.

00:56:46.890 --> 00:56:49.829
And that's kind of a way to start representing
those kinds of things.

00:56:49.829 --> 00:56:50.950
Tim Ferriss: All right.

00:56:50.950 --> 00:56:54.980
I'm going to ask a number of questions that
will no doubt put me at risk of embarrassing

00:56:54.980 --> 00:56:57.579
myself, but I knew that I when I —
Stephen Wolfram: I have to say one more thing.

00:56:57.579 --> 00:56:59.680
Tim Ferriss: Oh, yeah, just —
Stephen Wolfram: Because you were asking about

00:56:59.680 --> 00:57:03.150
languages and you were asking about different
human languages.

00:57:03.150 --> 00:57:07.640
That's an example of being in different places
in rulial space.

00:57:07.640 --> 00:57:13.400
So you can imagine two languages where the
way of thinking about the world is very similar,

00:57:13.400 --> 00:57:19.450
they kind of correspond to nearby places in
rulial space, where it's pretty easy to translate,

00:57:19.450 --> 00:57:23.660
to travel from one to the other, whereas things
which are very different, very different sort

00:57:23.660 --> 00:57:26.390
of views of the world are further away in
rulial space.

00:57:26.390 --> 00:57:30.190
And that's just a way of perhaps conceptualizing
what this thing is about.

00:57:30.190 --> 00:57:31.190
Please, go ahead.

00:57:31.190 --> 00:57:34.500
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, when you said that, I was
just thinking about gendered languages versus

00:57:34.500 --> 00:57:40.359
gendered languages or certain languages that
don't conjugate, say, past tense, like Chinese,

00:57:40.359 --> 00:57:46.390
Mandarin, and how that affects maybe where
you stand in rulial space.

00:57:46.390 --> 00:57:53.000
So how does a branching mind perceive a branching
universe or the branching might perceiving

00:57:53.000 --> 00:57:54.030
the branching universe?

00:57:54.030 --> 00:58:02.390
I think as many people hear this, they imagine
these multiple or infinite possibilities branching

00:58:02.390 --> 00:58:10.670
out in some form of to conjure the image of
the eddy, so these changing atoms, but if

00:58:10.670 --> 00:58:15.089
you took a snapshot of the eddy, minute after
minute, it would have some resemblance.

00:58:15.089 --> 00:58:20.210
But there is a branching that I think for
many folks listening will take place in linear

00:58:20.210 --> 00:58:24.559
time, and so there's some past to future to
this branching.

00:58:24.559 --> 00:58:31.970
I have tried to stretch the boundaries of
how I consider or define time by reading and

00:58:31.970 --> 00:58:35.950
listening to say, Carlo Rovelli, who I think
focuses a fair amount on quantum gravity.

00:58:35.950 --> 00:58:38.280
I don't know his research very well.

00:58:38.280 --> 00:58:41.410
How do you think about time?

00:58:41.410 --> 00:58:47.250
Is how humans experience or think about time
Just a very convenient collective delusion

00:58:47.250 --> 00:58:51.000
in terms of its linear past to future nature?

00:58:51.000 --> 00:58:53.930
Stephen Wolfram: So I mean, first thing is,
what is time?

00:58:53.930 --> 00:58:55.319
Tim Ferriss: Exactly.

00:58:55.319 --> 00:58:59.700
Stephen Wolfram: That's something that I think
we really kind of nailed in the way we think

00:58:59.700 --> 00:59:01.760
about our theory of physics.

00:59:01.760 --> 00:59:06.039
I mean, time is the inexorable progress of
computation.

00:59:06.039 --> 00:59:11.859
So in other words, the universe is in some
state, then the universe is going to be transformed

00:59:11.859 --> 00:59:14.960
to another state, and another one.

00:59:14.960 --> 00:59:19.270
That progressive process of transformation
is the passage of time.

00:59:19.270 --> 00:59:23.270
And this phenomenon of computational reducibility
that I was mentioning before that you kind

00:59:23.270 --> 00:59:27.960
of can't jump ahead, that is the fact that
time is meaningful.

00:59:27.960 --> 00:59:32.730
There is something, you can't just say, "Oh,
I didn't have to go through those moments

00:59:32.730 --> 00:59:33.730
of time.

00:59:33.730 --> 00:59:35.569
I could always just jump ahead."

00:59:35.569 --> 00:59:38.580
Now, in most of the universe, time is just
progressing.

00:59:38.580 --> 00:59:44.160
It's just as the universe is sort of updated,
so that corresponds to the passage of time.

00:59:44.160 --> 00:59:48.319
Now, we are part of the universe, so we are
being updated too.

00:59:48.319 --> 00:59:53.970
If the universe just stopped, we wouldn't
know it had stopped because we'd be stopped

00:59:53.970 --> 00:59:55.340
too.

00:59:55.340 --> 00:59:59.980
So for example, one place where that happens,
in the simplest kind of black hole, at the

00:59:59.980 --> 01:00:04.380
center of the simplest kind of black hole,
is the space-time singularity, which has the

01:00:04.380 --> 01:00:07.510
property that it's a place where time stops.

01:00:07.510 --> 01:00:12.010
And so in our model of physics, what's happening
is this universe is being updated, this network

01:00:12.010 --> 01:00:16.640
is being updated, being updated, but if you're
at the center of the black hole, it just stops.

01:00:16.640 --> 01:00:19.470
There's no more update that can be applied.

01:00:19.470 --> 01:00:23.800
Actually, if you're doing math, you kind of
want to get to that point.

01:00:23.800 --> 01:00:26.450
If you're doing a calculation, you do, "Oh,
we're calculating.

01:00:26.450 --> 01:00:30.789
These things are happening," and eventually,
you get to the answer and that's a place where

01:00:30.789 --> 01:00:32.950
it's fixed, nothing changes anymore.

01:00:32.950 --> 01:00:35.070
That's what happens at the center of a black
hole.

01:00:35.070 --> 01:00:40.309
It's kind of bad news in a sense, if you want
to have a future, so to speak, because time

01:00:40.309 --> 01:00:43.849
just stopped.

01:00:43.849 --> 01:00:51.960
So time, as far as I'm concerned, is this
inexorable progress of computation and time,

01:00:51.960 --> 01:00:56.710
in the actual way that it manifests in the
universe, has many complicated features.

01:00:56.710 --> 01:01:02.860
So for example, in relativity and gravitation
theory and so on, there are all kinds of ways

01:01:02.860 --> 01:01:07.349
in which the notion of, "When is it the same
time as somewhere else?"

01:01:07.349 --> 01:01:08.549
Is complicated.

01:01:08.549 --> 01:01:15.270
Like we say, let's say, have a Mars colony
one day and we define Earth standard time.

01:01:15.270 --> 01:01:17.540
Okay, it's 12 noon at this point.

01:01:17.540 --> 01:01:21.380
Well, it's 20 light minutes away to Mars,
for example.

01:01:21.380 --> 01:01:30.339
Do we say that the 12 noon is the time when
the light signal from our clock that said

01:01:30.339 --> 01:01:32.420
it was 12 noon on Earth reaches Mars?

01:01:32.420 --> 01:01:38.670
Or do we try and back calculate that and say,
"Well, it's the time when it would've reached

01:01:38.670 --> 01:01:40.540
if the clock had been 20 minutes early?"

01:01:40.540 --> 01:01:41.540
And so on.

01:01:41.540 --> 01:01:46.190
That whole question of the way in which you
kind of put these slices across the universe

01:01:46.190 --> 01:01:51.589
to define what counts as simultaneity in time,
that's kind of the story of relativity theory

01:01:51.589 --> 01:01:53.890
and gravitation theory and so on.

01:01:53.890 --> 01:01:57.900
And that's another kind of twist in this whole
thing.

01:01:57.900 --> 01:02:01.690
But in quantum mechanics, the big issue is,
is there just one threat— thread of time

01:02:01.690 --> 01:02:03.270
or are there many threads of time?

01:02:03.270 --> 01:02:07.400
Now, we humans normally only perceive one
thread of time.

01:02:07.400 --> 01:02:12.880
I've sort of wondered whether there's some
trance that people can go into that's kind

01:02:12.880 --> 01:02:18.119
of a multi-way trance, where they actually
have multiple threads of experience that are

01:02:18.119 --> 01:02:19.660
going on at the same time.

01:02:19.660 --> 01:02:24.690
But for most of us, most of the time, it's
just there's a definite thread of experience

01:02:24.690 --> 01:02:25.690
that we have.

01:02:25.690 --> 01:02:27.250
And that's a —
Tim Ferriss: If I may interrupt for a second,

01:02:27.250 --> 01:02:31.440
what prompted that wonder or question about
whether there are people through —

01:02:31.440 --> 01:02:35.570
Stephen Wolfram: Because one of the features
of our model of physics is what ultimately

01:02:35.570 --> 01:02:40.630
drives the mathematical structure of quantum
mechanics, is this assumption that we have

01:02:40.630 --> 01:02:45.799
that we are persistent in time and that we
can conflate things to the point where we

01:02:45.799 --> 01:02:47.640
have a single thread of experience.

01:02:47.640 --> 01:02:54.289
If that isn't the case, then we've got a different
theory of quantum mechanics, because quantum

01:02:54.289 --> 01:02:59.300
mechanics ends up being something which says,
"You do all this quantum mechanical stuff,

01:02:59.300 --> 01:03:01.080
it has these many parts of history."

01:03:01.080 --> 01:03:04.240
But in the end, we want to get an answer.

01:03:04.240 --> 01:03:07.339
We don't want to be saying we've got two different
answers in mind.

01:03:07.339 --> 01:03:11.400
We're going to say, "We say a definite thing
happened."

01:03:11.400 --> 01:03:16.880
And so for example, when people talk about
making quantum computers, the big thing that

01:03:16.880 --> 01:03:23.280
one hopes for is that one can use these multiple
threads of history to each run a different

01:03:23.280 --> 01:03:26.220
computation, and so then, you can do all these
things in parallel.

01:03:26.220 --> 01:03:30.870
Now the big problem, and again, I seem to
be mentioning Dick Feynman too much here,

01:03:30.870 --> 01:03:33.680
but he and I worked on quantum physics —
Tim Ferriss: Doesn't bother me.

01:03:33.680 --> 01:03:36.119
Stephen Wolfram: — back in 1981 or so.

01:03:36.119 --> 01:03:41.250
It was kind of a funny experience because
he did all his calculation by hand, and I

01:03:41.250 --> 01:03:42.450
was using a computer.

01:03:42.450 --> 01:03:47.450
I actually found one of the computations that
I did from that just recently, and he would

01:03:47.450 --> 01:03:51.750
do these calculations by hand, and I had no
idea why the answers he got were right.

01:03:51.750 --> 01:03:55.089
Because it's just like you do this calculation
and it's like, "You could have done this or

01:03:55.089 --> 01:03:57.900
this or this, you could have made this or
this assumption at this point.

01:03:57.900 --> 01:04:00.210
I don't know why that assumption is right."

01:04:00.210 --> 01:04:05.349
And he'd look at the stuff I did on a computer
and he'd say, "I have no idea why any of that's

01:04:05.349 --> 01:04:06.349
right."

01:04:06.349 --> 01:04:08.740
So that was an interesting challenge, so to
speak.

01:04:08.740 --> 01:04:14.770
But actually, even at that time, we kind of
concluded that the big question about making

01:04:14.770 --> 01:04:20.010
use of quantum mechanics to compute things
is, "How do you determine the answer?"

01:04:20.010 --> 01:04:24.430
In the formal theory of quantum mechanics,
how does the measurement work in quantum mechanics?

01:04:24.430 --> 01:04:28.720
How do you actually measure what happened
in the quantum process?

01:04:28.720 --> 01:04:33.549
Well now, what we see is there are all these
threads of history, and at the end, us humans,

01:04:33.549 --> 01:04:37.940
if we want to get a definite answer, have
to knit together all those threads of history.

01:04:37.940 --> 01:04:41.160
And the big question is, "How hard is it to
knit together those threads of history?"

01:04:41.160 --> 01:04:46.060
And if it's as hard to knit them together
as what you gain by having multiple threads,

01:04:46.060 --> 01:04:48.609
then you don't get an advantage in the end.

01:04:48.609 --> 01:04:52.559
And that's a difficult thing to figure out,
and it's something we're trying to figure

01:04:52.559 --> 01:04:53.559
out.

01:04:53.559 --> 01:04:56.660
I'm not hopeful about the true quantum advantage.

01:04:56.660 --> 01:05:00.700
I think that the formalism of quantum mechanics
is super interesting.

01:05:00.700 --> 01:05:06.539
And it's very, this whole idea of these, what
we call multiway graphs and this whole multiple

01:05:06.539 --> 01:05:08.410
threads of history and so on.

01:05:08.410 --> 01:05:12.859
That's a very interesting formal thing relevant
to many fields.

01:05:12.859 --> 01:05:15.900
But this idea that you're actually going to
be able to make an engineering system out

01:05:15.900 --> 01:05:19.690
of it and get this sort of quantum advantage,
less convincing.

01:05:19.690 --> 01:05:25.079
It's also, as a practical matter, the whole
quantum computing effort has caused people

01:05:25.079 --> 01:05:29.359
to think, "Oh, can we make computers out of
things other than electronics and semiconductors

01:05:29.359 --> 01:05:30.359
and so on?"

01:05:30.359 --> 01:05:32.150
And that's a completely worthwhile thing,
as well.

01:05:32.150 --> 01:05:33.410
So the two ends are worthwhile.

01:05:33.410 --> 01:05:37.050
I'm not sure that the middle is so worthwhile.

01:05:37.050 --> 01:05:38.300
So that's our notion of time.

01:05:38.300 --> 01:05:46.610
Now, in terms of people's perception of time,
it is this process of, we are undergoing these

01:05:46.610 --> 01:05:51.550
computations, our minds are undergoing these
computations, and so is the rest of the universe.

01:05:51.550 --> 01:05:55.819
And that's kind of, it's the alignment of
the computations going on in our minds with

01:05:55.819 --> 01:06:02.000
the computation going on in the universe that
leads these different forms of time, the time

01:06:02.000 --> 01:06:06.990
in thermodynamics of things sort of decaying
down to heat, or the time in the expansion

01:06:06.990 --> 01:06:10.079
of the universe, things about cosmology and
so on.

01:06:10.079 --> 01:06:14.760
The fact that all those different arrows of
time align is a consequence of the fact that

01:06:14.760 --> 01:06:19.510
they're actually the same thing, they're all
just the sort of inexorable process of computation

01:06:19.510 --> 01:06:20.890
that's happening in the universe.

01:06:20.890 --> 01:06:27.880
Tim Ferriss: I'm going to use a term that
is, might be frustratingly undefined, overused

01:06:27.880 --> 01:06:30.970
to the point that it's often undefined, at
least.

01:06:30.970 --> 01:06:36.940
But I'm going to ask this question anyway,
which is, do you have any thoughts on what

01:06:36.940 --> 01:06:38.950
constitutes consciousness?

01:06:38.950 --> 01:06:43.030
It can be defined any way you want, or it
can just be tossed.

01:06:43.030 --> 01:06:48.690
Or if that is an emergent property or a subjective
experience with certain underpinnings that

01:06:48.690 --> 01:06:50.770
can be currently explained?

01:06:50.770 --> 01:06:52.990
How do you think about, if at all, this may
be a terrible question —

01:06:52.990 --> 01:06:53.990
Stephen Wolfram: Yeah.

01:06:53.990 --> 01:06:57.420
Well, I've always sort of avoided it because
it's always seemed like a deeply slippery

01:06:57.420 --> 01:06:58.420
thing.

01:06:58.420 --> 01:07:03.329
But I was literally confronted with, "I need
to apply the idea of consciousness."

01:07:03.329 --> 01:07:05.980
I may not have to — and here's how.

01:07:05.980 --> 01:07:11.790
In the universe, ultimately there are all
these possible computations that can happen,

01:07:11.790 --> 01:07:16.029
but our minds don't do all possible computations.

01:07:16.029 --> 01:07:19.170
Our minds are somewhat more, they're much
more filtered in what they do.

01:07:19.170 --> 01:07:24.730
And in particular, they have these features
of computational boundedness, belief, and

01:07:24.730 --> 01:07:26.040
persistence and so on.

01:07:26.040 --> 01:07:34.490
For me, those are the things we need to use
about consciousness to derive things in physics.

01:07:34.490 --> 01:07:38.740
So those are features of consciousness that
distinguish us from the rest of the universe.

01:07:38.740 --> 01:07:43.490
It's kind of actually a little disappointing
because we might have thought, "Oh, there's

01:07:43.490 --> 01:07:47.700
inanimate matter and there's this and that,
and we've got this big stack we're building

01:07:47.700 --> 01:07:48.700
and it goes through life.

01:07:48.700 --> 01:07:51.050
And eventually, we get to intelligence, consciousness.

01:07:51.050 --> 01:07:52.760
We are the tippy-top.

01:07:52.760 --> 01:07:55.090
We are the best thing in the universe," so
to speak.

01:07:55.090 --> 01:07:59.490
But actually, what I've come to realize is
that that's not true at all, that the universe

01:07:59.490 --> 01:08:02.450
has much more capability than we have.

01:08:02.450 --> 01:08:06.970
And this thing we call consciousness is a
filtering of that capability to something

01:08:06.970 --> 01:08:11.020
specific, where we believe, for example, that
there's a single thread of experience that

01:08:11.020 --> 01:08:12.020
we have.

01:08:12.020 --> 01:08:19.170
And that that's kind of the thing that consciousness,
the application of consciousness to science

01:08:19.170 --> 01:08:25.120
is this thing where it's not about everything
in the universe, it's just about the particular

01:08:25.120 --> 01:08:31.020
things that are sort of the way that our minds
perceive things.

01:08:31.020 --> 01:08:35.909
I think an exercise people talk about, "Well,
how can you talk about this in this kind of

01:08:35.909 --> 01:08:38.290
very materialistic way?

01:08:38.290 --> 01:08:43.219
Isn't there some magic thing in consciousness
that is this sort of spark that is different

01:08:43.219 --> 01:08:44.589
from everything else in the universe?"

01:08:44.589 --> 01:08:48.460
Well, to us, inside, there absolutely is.

01:08:48.460 --> 01:08:55.120
To us, inside, we are this one point in rulial
space where there is this set of things going

01:08:55.120 --> 01:08:56.120
on.

01:08:56.120 --> 01:08:59.429
That is our experience of the universe, and
it's completely unique.

01:08:59.429 --> 01:09:04.150
And there may be some other point in rulial
space, some other mind that is fairly close

01:09:04.150 --> 01:09:06.870
by where we can say, "We're experiencing these
things.

01:09:06.870 --> 01:09:09.790
We can tell that they're similar to what's
being experienced here."

01:09:09.790 --> 01:09:13.430
But each sort of consciousness is unique in
that sense.

01:09:13.430 --> 01:09:19.000
Now, I was kind of doing an exercise recently,
which I need to finish, which is to describe

01:09:19.000 --> 01:09:21.540
what it's like to be a computer.

01:09:21.540 --> 01:09:29.109
And you imagine we humans, we live our lives,
we remember a bunch of stuff through our lives.

01:09:29.109 --> 01:09:32.650
Eventually, it's all lost when we die.

01:09:32.650 --> 01:09:38.210
And the question is, for a computer, from
the time it boots up to the time the operating

01:09:38.210 --> 01:09:44.880
system crashes, that's a period of time over
which the computer has sort of its life experiences.

01:09:44.880 --> 01:09:50.859
And how do those life experiences compare
to the "life experiences" that we humans have?

01:09:50.859 --> 01:09:56.540
There is a whole inner thinking that's going
on for the computer.

01:09:56.540 --> 01:09:58.220
How does that compare with us humans?

01:09:58.220 --> 01:10:03.130
There's sort of the communication with other
computers, the experience of the outside world

01:10:03.130 --> 01:10:04.440
and so on.

01:10:04.440 --> 01:10:05.560
How does that compare?

01:10:05.560 --> 01:10:11.790
How can we describe that in what it's like
to be — even a current computer, forget

01:10:11.790 --> 01:10:15.860
of the science fiction AI of the future, just
talk about a current computer.

01:10:15.860 --> 01:10:21.190
What would it be like to be the sort of inside
that, experiencing things from the point of

01:10:21.190 --> 01:10:23.250
view of the machine, so to speak?

01:10:23.250 --> 01:10:26.960
Tim Ferriss: Let's segue to personal productivity.

01:10:26.960 --> 01:10:33.840
So this is something I imagine you do still
think about a fair amount, and I've read a

01:10:33.840 --> 01:10:37.520
fair amount of your writing on personal infrastructure
hacks and so on.

01:10:37.520 --> 01:10:44.699
And it seems like there are, is I think you
might describe them, nerdy productivity hacks

01:10:44.699 --> 01:10:51.630
that then later become more mainstream or
more accepted, more widely distributed.

01:10:51.630 --> 01:10:58.650
Are there any personal productivity or infrastructure
tools or hacks that you are using now that

01:10:58.650 --> 01:11:04.219
you think will gain more adoption in some
form in the not-so-distant future?

01:11:04.219 --> 01:11:12.630
Stephen Wolfram: So I started live streaming
a bunch of working meetings that I do, and

01:11:12.630 --> 01:11:15.550
started this about, when was it?

01:11:15.550 --> 01:11:18.110
2017, so a few years ago.

01:11:18.110 --> 01:11:24.580
And it's really an interesting process because
a large fraction of software design reviews

01:11:24.580 --> 01:11:27.000
that we do are live streamed.

01:11:27.000 --> 01:11:35.090
And that means people who are in the meeting
are a little bit more paying attention because

01:11:35.090 --> 01:11:39.080
they kind of know it's going out to the world,
but it's also really nice because there's

01:11:39.080 --> 01:11:42.810
kind of an immediate feedback.

01:11:42.810 --> 01:11:46.210
If we announce some particular topic we're
going to work on, we'll get — some world

01:11:46.210 --> 01:11:50.750
experts in that topic often will show up because,
gosh, they know they're going to be stuck

01:11:50.750 --> 01:11:55.360
using our tools and they might as well contribute
to getting them to be designed right.

01:11:55.360 --> 01:11:59.190
And also, people who are just energetic users
about technology.

01:11:59.190 --> 01:12:02.690
And it's really a wonderful kind of immediate
feedback.

01:12:02.690 --> 01:12:11.389
To me it's a little bit, it helps me feel
that the time I'm spending doing very detailed,

01:12:11.389 --> 01:12:16.790
grinding away, trying to figure out how software
should be designed and so on, it feels like

01:12:16.790 --> 01:12:21.000
the fact that I'm leaving some record of this,
feels like it makes it more meaningful.

01:12:21.000 --> 01:12:25.540
And I know people have used some of our live
streams for software engineering classes and

01:12:25.540 --> 01:12:26.540
things like this.

01:12:26.540 --> 01:12:32.800
And so it helps me make it seem more meaningful,
so to speak.

01:12:32.800 --> 01:12:39.469
The even more extreme version of that is that
I've been doing video work logs, which means

01:12:39.469 --> 01:12:44.410
I'm just sitting by myself and I'm working
on something and I'm writing some document

01:12:44.410 --> 01:12:48.750
or something like that, and I just record
it.

01:12:48.750 --> 01:12:53.020
Like last night, I probably recorded five
hours of video work logs.

01:12:53.020 --> 01:12:56.980
Basically was working on this sort of personal
journey history thing, and I'm recording my

01:12:56.980 --> 01:12:59.580
screen and I'm just recording what I'm doing.

01:12:59.580 --> 01:13:01.489
Now, why am I doing this?

01:13:01.489 --> 01:13:05.250
Because it's easy to do and the thing that
— and somehow, it makes it feel a little

01:13:05.250 --> 01:13:06.719
bit more meaningful to me.

01:13:06.719 --> 01:13:10.920
It sort of makes me think, "Oh, I'm not going
to goof off in that way because I'm screen

01:13:10.920 --> 01:13:11.920
recording everything here."

01:13:11.920 --> 01:13:19.429
And okay, I admit, I have a secondary screen,
so I can goof off on the secondary screen.

01:13:19.429 --> 01:13:26.460
And the other thing is, when I'm doing science
stuff, what has happened, a number of times,

01:13:26.460 --> 01:13:30.139
is people say, "Well, how did you figure that
out?"

01:13:30.139 --> 01:13:34.190
I'm saying, "You can really find out how I
figured that out.

01:13:34.190 --> 01:13:35.662
Just go look at this video —
Tim Ferriss: Just watch it.

01:13:35.662 --> 01:13:38.480
Stephen Wolfram: — and you can find the
minute where I figured this out."

01:13:38.480 --> 01:13:43.290
And maybe I got it wrong, and you'll see,
"Look, he did something really stupid there."

01:13:43.290 --> 01:13:46.739
And you can see that moment, and you can then
unwind it.

01:13:46.739 --> 01:13:50.690
Same thing, by the way, for our software design
meetings.

01:13:50.690 --> 01:13:56.400
The people who do project management routinely
go back and look at all kinds of pieces of

01:13:56.400 --> 01:13:57.400
the meetings.

01:13:57.400 --> 01:13:58.400
"What actually happened?

01:13:58.400 --> 01:13:59.560
How did we decide this?

01:13:59.560 --> 01:14:02.690
Oh, a bug showed up there.

01:14:02.690 --> 01:14:07.240
Let's go carve that piece of the video out
and send it to people," and so on.

01:14:07.240 --> 01:14:15.690
So I think this thing about doing actual production
meetings, so to speak, live streamed is — I

01:14:15.690 --> 01:14:17.230
don't know anybody else who does that.

01:14:17.230 --> 01:14:19.200
I think nobody else is crazy enough to do
it.

01:14:19.200 --> 01:14:25.090
Stephen Wolfram: And part of why we can do
it is that we're in a unique place and technology

01:14:25.090 --> 01:14:28.980
space, where it's not like — I know people
who wanted to compete with us have watched

01:14:28.980 --> 01:14:29.980
live streams.

01:14:29.980 --> 01:14:30.980
It's like, good luck.

01:14:30.980 --> 01:14:33.440
We just spent 35 years building all this stuff.

01:14:33.440 --> 01:14:36.650
One live stream is not going to let you rebuild
that tower.

01:14:36.650 --> 01:14:37.810
So, that's one thing.

01:14:37.810 --> 01:14:43.870
The other thing that I myself have become
confident enough that even though I know I'm

01:14:43.870 --> 01:14:49.050
going to say really stupid things on these
live streams, I don't care.

01:14:49.050 --> 01:14:52.630
These are real life things where, yes, I'm
going to make mistakes.

01:14:52.630 --> 01:14:54.290
People are going to say, "You're wrong.

01:14:54.290 --> 01:14:55.290
You're wrong."

01:14:55.290 --> 01:15:00.150
And we're going to have a little argument,
and eventually, I'm going to say, "Hey, okay,

01:15:00.150 --> 01:15:01.150
you're right."

01:15:01.150 --> 01:15:07.120
And I don't have an internal ego issue with
either that process happening or that being

01:15:07.120 --> 01:15:09.861
something that people can go back and find
it.

01:15:09.861 --> 01:15:14.719
I'm sure it's possible to find all kinds of
terrible, stupid things that I said in these

01:15:14.719 --> 01:15:16.139
live streams and so on.

01:15:16.139 --> 01:15:17.139
Okay.

01:15:17.139 --> 01:15:21.010
So, another productivity hack, I suppose,
related to live streaming is this.

01:15:21.010 --> 01:15:26.910
So, at the beginning of the pandemic, I thought,
"Oh, all these kids are going to be out of

01:15:26.910 --> 01:15:27.910
school.

01:15:27.910 --> 01:15:30.190
I know a bunch of stuff about science."

01:15:30.190 --> 01:15:32.530
Every so often I'll do a Q&amp;A.

01:15:32.530 --> 01:15:38.150
Once a week I'll do a Q&amp;A about science and
tech for kids and others.

01:15:38.150 --> 01:15:41.750
So I started doing this at the beginning of
the pandemic, and I've still been doing it.

01:15:41.750 --> 01:15:48.989
I've done 100 episodes or more now of this
hour, hour and a half, on Fridays, of science

01:15:48.989 --> 01:15:50.450
and technology Q&amp;A for kids and others.

01:15:50.450 --> 01:15:53.540
Okay, so what has that done for me?

01:15:53.540 --> 01:15:58.610
People ask all kinds of crazy things, and
it makes me think about stuff.

01:15:58.610 --> 01:16:05.590
And I find that this process of I'm just sitting
here, looking at the camera and no notes,

01:16:05.590 --> 01:16:09.520
no looking anything up, just how can I figure
out the answer to that question.

01:16:09.520 --> 01:16:14.530
And from a couple of days ago, there was a
question somebody asked that made me realize

01:16:14.530 --> 01:16:18.489
some piece of physics that is relevant to
our Physics Project, that I'd never realized

01:16:18.489 --> 01:16:19.510
before.

01:16:19.510 --> 01:16:25.130
And the process of explaining it, particularly
with the forced feature that I'm just going

01:16:25.130 --> 01:16:31.469
to go on talking at the camera, so to speak,
it's that forcing function of, "So now you've

01:16:31.469 --> 01:16:36.210
got to figure something out," I found really
useful for understanding things.

01:16:36.210 --> 01:16:41.340
And I've been doing actually two other alternating,
alternate weeks Q&amp;As, one about history of

01:16:41.340 --> 01:16:46.830
science and technology and the other about
business innovation and managing life, more

01:16:46.830 --> 01:16:47.830
your territory.

01:16:47.830 --> 01:16:56.270
And people there, again, I just find it really
helpful in crystallizing my own thinking that

01:16:56.270 --> 01:17:01.370
I'm trying to answer these questions, particularly
in this real-time format, where I don't get

01:17:01.370 --> 01:17:05.540
to say, "Oh, let me think about that, and
let me think some more about it and so on."

01:17:05.540 --> 01:17:07.320
And then I never get around to answering it.

01:17:07.320 --> 01:17:12.770
I'm kind of on the spot and having to answer
something, and that's been a very useful process.

01:17:12.770 --> 01:17:19.230
I know that, in terms of explaining things,
science things, I'm pretty sure I can feel

01:17:19.230 --> 01:17:24.530
that I've gotten better through hundreds of
hours of people ask random questions, and

01:17:24.530 --> 01:17:25.670
I try and answer them.

01:17:25.670 --> 01:17:29.739
The one thing that always trips me up is when
somebody asks a question, and I think to myself,

01:17:29.739 --> 01:17:30.860
"That's really easy.

01:17:30.860 --> 01:17:32.920
I have that one absolutely nailed."

01:17:32.920 --> 01:17:34.820
Those are the ones that I trip up on.

01:17:34.820 --> 01:17:38.920
And actually, there's a curious reason, which
I did realize about that, which is a lot of

01:17:38.920 --> 01:17:42.949
these things, where it's like, I've understood
that since I was 12 years old.

01:17:42.949 --> 01:17:48.770
So it's easy, and then I realize, oh, my gosh,
it relies on this thing and that thing and

01:17:48.770 --> 01:17:53.310
the other thing, which, yeah, I learnt when
I was 12, 13 years old or something.

01:17:53.310 --> 01:17:55.790
But not everybody knows that, and I have to
go explain that.

01:17:55.790 --> 01:17:59.530
And I have to go explain this other thing
that I thought was obvious, and pretty soon,

01:17:59.530 --> 01:18:03.000
one's descending into the swamp of complicated
stuff.

01:18:03.000 --> 01:18:10.199
Tim Ferriss: So let's look at maybe, if there
is a there, there, we'll find out, some of

01:18:10.199 --> 01:18:13.190
the physiological underpinnings of productivity.

01:18:13.190 --> 01:18:19.790
So, we have these physical bodies, and you
seem to, this could just be my perception,

01:18:19.790 --> 01:18:23.090
but have energy reserves.

01:18:23.090 --> 01:18:28.800
I'm not using energy in a very precise, physics
way but more in a metabolic way.

01:18:28.800 --> 01:18:29.800
Right?

01:18:29.800 --> 01:18:32.960
You're producing enough neurotransmitters,
and you store enough glycogen and so on that

01:18:32.960 --> 01:18:39.060
you are able to maintain, it seems like, a
very high rate of output.

01:18:39.060 --> 01:18:41.500
Do you think about energy management in that
context?

01:18:41.500 --> 01:18:44.730
Stephen Wolfram: You know what —
Tim Ferriss: Or is that something that just

01:18:44.730 --> 01:18:48.250
comes so naturally out of the box that you
just have that advantage, and you don't have

01:18:48.250 --> 01:18:49.330
to think about it?

01:18:49.330 --> 01:18:52.219
Stephen Wolfram: I think I'm lucky that I'm
fairly energetic.

01:18:52.219 --> 01:18:58.940
And it's always amusing to me that I'm an
old guy, now, and I can out energize lots

01:18:58.940 --> 01:19:00.980
of young folk who are working with me and
so on.

01:19:00.980 --> 01:19:03.171
And that always — Actually, I get a kick
out of being able to do that.

01:19:03.171 --> 01:19:04.800
So that —
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, why not?

01:19:04.800 --> 01:19:08.580
Stephen Wolfram: — that helps add energy
to the whole picture.

01:19:08.580 --> 01:19:14.020
But no, I think for me, look, one thing is
that I do things that I like to do.

01:19:14.020 --> 01:19:17.870
And for me, that's a huge, energizing force.

01:19:17.870 --> 01:19:21.850
If I was like, "Oh, gosh, I have to do this,
and I don't really want to do it, and I'm

01:19:21.850 --> 01:19:27.500
not very interested," and it's like I'm jumping
into things, where I really want to do this.

01:19:27.500 --> 01:19:31.920
It's like I was — okay, another strange
thing I just did, I seem to have been reliving

01:19:31.920 --> 01:19:39.640
my life 50 years ago, in the last few weeks,
because I decided to organize, a couple months

01:19:39.640 --> 01:19:45.920
ago, I initiated a reunion for my elementary
school graduating class, which was 1972.

01:19:45.920 --> 01:19:51.110
And so, it's a small subgroup of people, and
so it was the 50-year follow up.

01:19:51.110 --> 01:19:55.500
And actually, it was, I liked these people
50 years ago.

01:19:55.500 --> 01:19:56.500
I liked them 50 years later.

01:19:56.500 --> 01:19:58.210
That was nice.

01:19:58.210 --> 01:20:02.480
But I realized one of the things that's a
little disappointing is some fraction of these

01:20:02.480 --> 01:20:07.390
people were — we were writing little blurbs
about what we've been doing, and they're like,

01:20:07.390 --> 01:20:08.449
"I retired."

01:20:08.449 --> 01:20:10.330
"I retired," etc, etc, etc.

01:20:10.330 --> 01:20:15.980
And they're British folk, so they had lots
of witty things to say about what they were

01:20:15.980 --> 01:20:17.159
doing in their retirement.

01:20:17.159 --> 01:20:23.880
But so, I was realizing, I'm not retired,
and I'm not even — and I realized I'd been

01:20:23.880 --> 01:20:29.179
working more than 12 hours a day, every day,
for basically all of the last 50 years.

01:20:29.179 --> 01:20:35.270
And I'm having a good time, and I've been
lucky enough to be able to mostly do things

01:20:35.270 --> 01:20:41.520
that add energy to me rather than taking it
away.

01:20:41.520 --> 01:20:47.130
When I do creative things, and I figure stuff
out and even write things and so on, that

01:20:47.130 --> 01:20:52.980
the process of finishing them and getting
them done is energizing to me.

01:20:52.980 --> 01:21:00.490
And I think, if I was doing things where I'm
like, oh, I don't know, back in the day, before

01:21:00.490 --> 01:21:04.560
we realized this just didn't work, I would
occasionally go shopping with my wife.

01:21:04.560 --> 01:21:05.560
Okay.

01:21:05.560 --> 01:21:06.560
Wrong [inaudible 01:36:00].

01:21:06.560 --> 01:21:07.560
Tim Ferriss: Uh-huh.

01:21:07.560 --> 01:21:08.560
Stephen Wolfram: Right?

01:21:08.560 --> 01:21:09.560
And I would try and take my wife to science
museums.

01:21:09.560 --> 01:21:10.560
Okay.

01:21:10.560 --> 01:21:14.170
So, my wife is a mathematician, so science
museums are not so far away, but they're far

01:21:14.170 --> 01:21:18.370
enough away that she was like, "Oh, I'm getting
so tired walking around this science museum."

01:21:18.370 --> 01:21:22.650
So, for me, it would have a physiological
effect on me, going around —

01:21:22.650 --> 01:21:23.650
Tim Ferriss: Shopping.

01:21:23.650 --> 01:21:25.240
Stephen Wolfram: — looking around shops.

01:21:25.240 --> 01:21:28.500
I'm like, I feel really tired.

01:21:28.500 --> 01:21:30.940
I have to —
Tim Ferriss: Getting so sleepy.

01:21:30.940 --> 01:21:33.239
Stephen Wolfram: — lay down, etc, etc, etc.

01:21:33.239 --> 01:21:37.410
So, I think the effect that I'm doing things
I really want to be doing is an important

01:21:37.410 --> 01:21:38.440
effect.

01:21:38.440 --> 01:21:45.949
Now, having said that, I did discover, when
I was 40 years old, hey, you should do some

01:21:45.949 --> 01:21:51.380
exercise, and that helped for me, I think,
add a bunch of energy.

01:21:51.380 --> 01:21:55.469
For the last three years, I've walked more
than 10,000 steps every single day, for the

01:21:55.469 --> 01:21:58.139
last three years, and that's just one of my
constraints.

01:21:58.139 --> 01:22:03.860
Even if I'm traveling or this or that or the
other, the people who schedule stuff for me,

01:22:03.860 --> 01:22:05.700
it's like I've got to walk 10,000 steps.

01:22:05.700 --> 01:22:10.650
If that's around an airport on phone calls
and things, so be it, but that's a constraint

01:22:10.650 --> 01:22:13.530
on my life, so to speak.

01:22:13.530 --> 01:22:15.429
And I think that has had a positive effect.

01:22:15.429 --> 01:22:20.920
For me, it's been bizarre because, when I
was younger, I wasn't in terribly good shape.

01:22:20.920 --> 01:22:22.940
I'm in better shape now.

01:22:22.940 --> 01:22:28.910
And so, for me, I don't yet notice that I'm
aging because I'm actually better off, in

01:22:28.910 --> 01:22:30.560
many ways, than I was when I was younger.

01:22:30.560 --> 01:22:33.960
Tim Ferriss: Your mitochondria are getting
younger, are rejuvenating.

01:22:33.960 --> 01:22:35.040
Stephen Wolfram: Yeah, yeah.

01:22:35.040 --> 01:22:36.040
Right.

01:22:36.040 --> 01:22:40.020
No, another thing about that and about the
management of one's life and so on, I've been

01:22:40.020 --> 01:22:45.140
fortunate enough that, often, you'll do something,
and maybe you do something really cool when

01:22:45.140 --> 01:22:46.570
you're 25 years old.

01:22:46.570 --> 01:22:49.710
And then, it's all downhill from there, and
it's hard to get motivated.

01:22:49.710 --> 01:22:54.440
And I've been lucky enough that, for example,
this Physics Project that just arrived, three

01:22:54.440 --> 01:22:57.390
years ago, that was something I didn't really
expect.

01:22:57.390 --> 01:23:03.130
It's a rejuvenating thing, and it's just added
a lot of energy to my — it's just there's

01:23:03.130 --> 01:23:06.090
so many things to think about and so on.

01:23:06.090 --> 01:23:07.090
That's one thing.

01:23:07.090 --> 01:23:09.570
I sleep as close to eight hours as I can.

01:23:09.570 --> 01:23:15.870
I don't try and game it of saying I'm going
to try and shave off extra time and so on.

01:23:15.870 --> 01:23:20.560
I'm very habitual, in terms of when I go to
sleep, when I wake up.

01:23:20.560 --> 01:23:24.510
Things I eat tend to be habitual, and they
may not be optimal, but they're habitual at

01:23:24.510 --> 01:23:25.590
least.

01:23:25.590 --> 01:23:31.489
I've found that, for me, I try and optimize
away all those aspects of my life that I really

01:23:31.489 --> 01:23:38.159
don't care about, so to speak, that I don't
keep those as simple and not having to think

01:23:38.159 --> 01:23:46.550
about them as possible so that I can spend
my thinking effort on things where I really

01:23:46.550 --> 01:23:50.040
want to think about these things and really
want to spend my time on them.

01:23:50.040 --> 01:23:51.040
But, yeah.

01:23:51.040 --> 01:23:54.080
No, I've been fortunate enough that, for whatever
reason, the mitochondria are still alive and

01:23:54.080 --> 01:23:57.159
kicking and providing good energy.

01:23:57.159 --> 01:24:03.660
But as I say, I think it's mostly that just
doing things that I want to do, and also,

01:24:03.660 --> 01:24:07.860
I tend to organize it so that there are things
that I save up for.

01:24:07.860 --> 01:24:12.770
I'm going to do this if I'm feeling tired,
or I have another set of things.

01:24:12.770 --> 01:24:15.199
I'm going to do this if I get sick.

01:24:15.199 --> 01:24:16.290
Okay.

01:24:16.290 --> 01:24:17.740
And I've had a whole bunch of those.

01:24:17.740 --> 01:24:23.830
And I've only been sick once in the last three
years, so I didn't have a chance to — I've

01:24:23.830 --> 01:24:27.120
got this big, pent-up supply of things to
do when I'm sick.

01:24:27.120 --> 01:24:33.600
Tim Ferriss: Now, are those just low-energy,
low-interest tasks that nonetheless have to

01:24:33.600 --> 01:24:38.170
get done, like talk to my accountant about
X, Y, Z, whatever, something like that?

01:24:38.170 --> 01:24:39.170
Stephen Wolfram: Yeah.

01:24:39.170 --> 01:24:40.170
Yeah.

01:24:40.170 --> 01:24:42.310
Well, I'm not usually talking to other people,
but usually organizing informational kinds

01:24:42.310 --> 01:24:43.310
of things.

01:24:43.310 --> 01:24:44.310
Tim Ferriss: I see.

01:24:44.310 --> 01:24:45.310
I see.

01:24:45.310 --> 01:24:47.000
Stephen Wolfram: Or sometimes, they're…
watch this video that I've been meaning to

01:24:47.000 --> 01:24:49.900
watch and have never had time to do.

01:24:49.900 --> 01:24:58.570
But I did learn, okay, so this is one not
yet very scientific hack fact, which is, I

01:24:58.570 --> 01:25:02.260
was curious what has caused me to get sick
when I've gotten sick?

01:25:02.260 --> 01:25:03.870
Why have I gotten sick?

01:25:03.870 --> 01:25:10.320
And so I have the data for, I think, 27 years
maybe of, I think, all the times I've gotten

01:25:10.320 --> 01:25:11.320
sick.

01:25:11.320 --> 01:25:13.570
It's always upper respiratory things.

01:25:13.570 --> 01:25:14.810
Right?

01:25:14.810 --> 01:25:19.070
And so I think I've gotten sick 25 times in
27 years.

01:25:19.070 --> 01:25:23.080
And the question is, what was I doing when
I got sick?

01:25:23.080 --> 01:25:27.480
And was it, oh, I went out, and I met a bunch
of people, or was it whatever?

01:25:27.480 --> 01:25:31.361
And the one correlation, and I haven't been
completely scientific about this, the one

01:25:31.361 --> 01:25:39.410
correlation was it was often two days after
I was on a flight, on a plane.

01:25:39.410 --> 01:25:40.410
Okay.

01:25:40.410 --> 01:25:43.130
And in a few cases, that was not a commercial
plane.

01:25:43.130 --> 01:25:50.210
That was a private plane, so without a lot
of other people on it, and so it's interesting.

01:25:50.210 --> 01:25:55.820
And so then I asked my medical research friends
and so on, "Hey, what's going on here?"

01:25:55.820 --> 01:25:56.949
And here's the theory.

01:25:56.949 --> 01:26:03.280
The theory would be a big part of upper respiratory
defense, so to speak, is the innate immune

01:26:03.280 --> 01:26:06.710
system operating in one's nose and so on.

01:26:06.710 --> 01:26:13.380
And if you get your nose dried out and so
on, from being in the dry air and on planes

01:26:13.380 --> 01:26:18.300
and things like that, your little, innate
immune system doesn't stand a chance.

01:26:18.300 --> 01:26:25.330
So my hack has been take things like wheat
germ and so on that stimulate, just before

01:26:25.330 --> 01:26:31.560
I go on a plane, take that and a couple of
other things, and so far, we only have an

01:26:31.560 --> 01:26:34.290
N of about eight or something of trip side
time.

01:26:34.290 --> 01:26:39.150
So far, I haven't gotten sick
Tim Ferriss: So far, so good.

01:26:39.150 --> 01:26:45.460
It also makes me think of ways that you could,
not necessarily humidify, but maintain the

01:26:45.460 --> 01:26:52.730
sort of moisture integrity of the sinal lining,
as well, with a spray or something like that.

01:26:52.730 --> 01:26:54.080
Stephen Wolfram: Yeah, I thought about that.

01:26:54.080 --> 01:26:58.680
My most relevant medical research friend claimed
it's easier to just take choline than it is

01:26:58.680 --> 01:26:59.680
to try and do that.

01:26:59.680 --> 01:27:02.489
Tim Ferriss: Try to keep the nose well hydrated.

01:27:02.489 --> 01:27:03.489
Stephen Wolfram: Right.

01:27:03.489 --> 01:27:04.770
It's a strange thing.

01:27:04.770 --> 01:27:08.270
You're on some flight, going somewhere, and
you're continually stuffing things into your

01:27:08.270 --> 01:27:09.270
nose or whatever.

01:27:09.270 --> 01:27:11.010
No, that's —
Tim Ferriss: So, Stephen —

01:27:11.010 --> 01:27:16.380
Stephen Wolfram: One of the things I found
that, for me, it's keep the list of things

01:27:16.380 --> 01:27:22.230
to do when I'm tired because, for me, in terms
of motivation and so on, it's always nice.

01:27:22.230 --> 01:27:25.310
If I'm sick, I might be like, "Oh, my gosh,
I'm sick.

01:27:25.310 --> 01:27:26.350
That's so terrible."

01:27:26.350 --> 01:27:31.949
But in a sense, I'm like, "Great, now I have
a chance to do these things that I knew I

01:27:31.949 --> 01:27:32.949
had to do somehow."

01:27:32.949 --> 01:27:36.270
I do the same thing when I'm driving places.

01:27:36.270 --> 01:27:41.150
I always maintain a call-while-driving list
of phone calls that are slightly more, "I've

01:27:41.150 --> 01:27:42.150
got to do this sometime.

01:27:42.150 --> 01:27:44.440
I don't need to be in front of a computer."

01:27:44.440 --> 01:27:45.630
This is something I can do then.

01:27:45.630 --> 01:27:52.401
And it's actually good for lots of interactions
that I do, where I'd never get around to it.

01:27:52.401 --> 01:27:56.330
It's just like, if there's a person who lives
in the same city that you do, you never see

01:27:56.330 --> 01:27:57.330
them.

01:27:57.330 --> 01:28:01.630
But if they live somewhere completely different,
"Oh, I'm coming to wherever for a day," and

01:28:01.630 --> 01:28:02.630
you end up seeing them.

01:28:02.630 --> 01:28:05.920
And it's been the same thing for me with call-while-driving.

01:28:05.920 --> 01:28:11.290
It's like, "I'm going to call somebody," and
so this is that process.

01:28:11.290 --> 01:28:18.420
Tim Ferriss: Stephen, I'm so continually impressed
with not just the breadth of your thinking,

01:28:18.420 --> 01:28:25.909
but how you log and track and interpret so
much data.

01:28:25.909 --> 01:28:30.860
I think that I take a lot of notes, but you
mentioned, at the top of this conversation,

01:28:30.860 --> 01:28:33.870
a quarter million pages, something along those
lines.

01:28:33.870 --> 01:28:35.159
It's just incredible.

01:28:35.159 --> 01:28:37.340
Stephen Wolfram: That's the stuff on paper
actually.

01:28:37.340 --> 01:28:42.350
I have three million emails as well, so yeah,
a lot of stuff, over a long period of time.

01:28:42.350 --> 01:28:47.369
Tim Ferriss: So, you have a lot of stuff,
over a long period of time, and I would love

01:28:47.369 --> 01:28:49.100
to, at some point, do a round two.

01:28:49.100 --> 01:28:55.000
I'm sure we could do themed conversations
on probably several dozen different topics.

01:28:55.000 --> 01:28:59.850
Is there anything else that you would like
to mention, in this conversation, or call

01:28:59.850 --> 01:29:02.460
my audience's attention to —
Stephen Wolfram: Oh, my.

01:29:02.460 --> 01:29:07.550
Tim Ferriss: — anything at all, in terms
of closing remarks, comments, any grievances

01:29:07.550 --> 01:29:13.270
you'd like to air publicly, really anything
at all that you'd like to mention before we

01:29:13.270 --> 01:29:14.270
—
Stephen Wolfram: Makes me want to ask you

01:29:14.270 --> 01:29:18.730
for a bunch of personal productivity hacks
and so on and the what am I missing type thing

01:29:18.730 --> 01:29:20.179
—
Tim Ferriss: Oh, boy.

01:29:20.179 --> 01:29:21.179
Yeah.

01:29:21.179 --> 01:29:22.970
Stephen Wolfram: — because one slowly accumulates
these things.

01:29:22.970 --> 01:29:30.000
And I find I'll try things and probably two-thirds
of the things I try work, and one third don't.

01:29:30.000 --> 01:29:32.400
But it's keep trying them, but anyway.

01:29:32.400 --> 01:29:33.400
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:29:33.400 --> 01:29:36.310
Stephen Wolfram: So, I think, no, we've covered
all kinds of things.

01:29:36.310 --> 01:29:41.310
Tim Ferriss: I look forward to hopefully seeing
you again in person, at some point, but this

01:29:41.310 --> 01:29:43.960
has been delightful and very fun for me.

01:29:43.960 --> 01:29:48.770
I've taken copious notes, so I will be doing
lots of follow up on my own.

01:29:48.770 --> 01:29:51.199
And you seem to be doing pretty well on the
productivity side.

01:29:51.199 --> 01:29:56.500
If I think of anything that is a gross omission,
I will be sure to send it to you.

01:29:56.500 --> 01:29:57.690
Stephen Wolfram: Okay.

01:29:57.690 --> 01:29:58.690
Yeah.

01:29:58.690 --> 01:30:03.320
Tim Ferriss: People can find you on Twitter,
Stephen, that's a ph, Stephen_Wolfram, then

01:30:03.320 --> 01:30:09.830
Facebook/stephenwolfram, linkedin.com, also,
your name and then the website stephenwolfram.com.

01:30:09.830 --> 01:30:12.830
And we'll link to everything that you've mentioned.

01:30:12.830 --> 01:30:19.470
Is there anything that you would like to point
people to that is top of mind for you, at

01:30:19.470 --> 01:30:22.699
the moment, or any resources that people may
not find on their own?

01:30:22.699 --> 01:30:23.699
Stephen Wolfram: Well, let's see.

01:30:23.699 --> 01:30:26.760
The stuff I write ends up in writings.stephenwolfram.com.

01:30:26.760 --> 01:30:31.330
And I put lots of effort into writing these
things, so hopefully some people find them

01:30:31.330 --> 01:30:32.900
fun to read.

01:30:32.900 --> 01:30:37.030
Although even the process of writing them,
as I was explaining, is a useful process in

01:30:37.030 --> 01:30:38.030
its own right.

01:30:38.030 --> 01:30:46.190
There's also a recent thing, for me, is we
just launched our Wolfram Institute, that's,

01:30:46.190 --> 01:30:50.810
I suppose, an attempted productivity hack.

01:30:50.810 --> 01:30:57.480
My company, which I started now 36 years ago,
is my machine for turning ideas that I have

01:30:57.480 --> 01:30:58.900
into real things.

01:30:58.900 --> 01:31:04.370
And it's 800 people who are really, really
good at doing that and coming up with their

01:31:04.370 --> 01:31:05.860
own ideas as well.

01:31:05.860 --> 01:31:10.310
But that's been a thing, where mostly, we
make products, but one of the problems I've

01:31:10.310 --> 01:31:15.420
been trying to solve is, if you're making
basic science, what's the machine that does

01:31:15.420 --> 01:31:16.420
that?

01:31:16.420 --> 01:31:22.250
And I've carved off a bit of resources from
the company and so on, to do it, but we just

01:31:22.250 --> 01:31:28.660
recently launched Wolfram Institute, which
is a thing whose goal is to do basic science.

01:31:28.660 --> 01:31:36.880
And that's a new thing in the last just few
weeks, and so stay tuned for interesting things

01:31:36.880 --> 01:31:37.880
that are happening there.

01:31:37.880 --> 01:31:42.930
And I guess there'll be yet more live streaming
of science in action and so on there.

01:31:42.930 --> 01:31:45.000
So, those are a few things.

01:31:45.000 --> 01:31:50.670
And I suppose the other, I have to plug my
life work, which my life work is building

01:31:50.670 --> 01:31:55.480
Wolfram Language and Wolfram|Alpha and Mathematica
and so on, which are all part of the same

01:31:55.480 --> 01:31:58.940
idea of make the world computational.

01:31:58.940 --> 01:32:06.980
And I suppose the one pitch I would make is,
what we've built, I can inexorably see is

01:32:06.980 --> 01:32:08.420
an artifact from the future.

01:32:08.420 --> 01:32:13.550
And in other words, the direction that things
are going in, is going in this direction of

01:32:13.550 --> 01:32:17.370
representing the world computationally and
being able to really make use of that.

01:32:17.370 --> 01:32:22.890
But there are a few million people who actually
do make use of it in our technology stack,

01:32:22.890 --> 01:32:27.600
but there are a lot of millions of people
who don't and that this is an inexorable piece

01:32:27.600 --> 01:32:28.989
of the future.

01:32:28.989 --> 01:32:33.550
And it's a big advantage if you can grab the
magic from the future.

01:32:33.550 --> 01:32:40.500
I've done a lot of work with kids, who are
learning our stuff and doing projects and

01:32:40.500 --> 01:32:47.190
so on, and I've taken to referring to learning
computational language is a superpower.

01:32:47.190 --> 01:32:50.389
It's something that you get to do that, and
then you can do all kinds of magic things

01:32:50.389 --> 01:32:51.389
with it.

01:32:51.389 --> 01:32:54.510
Anyway, so learn that superpower, and more
people should do it.

01:32:54.510 --> 01:33:01.520
And it's one of these things where you can
kind of see, in the world, when things involve

01:33:01.520 --> 01:33:06.470
big ideas, there's a certain, inexorable slowness
to the way that they get adopted.

01:33:06.470 --> 01:33:11.780
And there are always some number of early
adopters, who are the ones that run out in

01:33:11.780 --> 01:33:18.420
front, and so my parting pitch would be, if
you don't understand computational language

01:33:18.420 --> 01:33:23.860
and Wolfram Language and so on, try to understand
it because it is, for me, the — you talk

01:33:23.860 --> 01:33:26.020
about productivity hacks.

01:33:26.020 --> 01:33:31.330
The biggest amplifier, hugest productivity
hack is the whole computational language idea.

01:33:31.330 --> 01:33:36.520
That's what all the things I've done in science
and in technology, they are all based on that

01:33:36.520 --> 01:33:40.580
idea and the tower of technology that we built
around that.

01:33:40.580 --> 01:33:44.710
So that's my parting, ultimate productivity
hack.

01:33:44.710 --> 01:33:45.930
Tim Ferriss: Wonderful.

01:33:45.930 --> 01:33:51.040
And for everybody listening, Mathematica,
Wolfram|Alpha, Wolfram Language, we will link

01:33:51.040 --> 01:33:53.320
to all of these things in the show notes,
at tim.blog/podcast.

01:33:53.320 --> 01:34:00.300
And you can just search Wolfram, W-O-L-F-R-A-M,
and that will pop right up.

01:34:00.300 --> 01:34:07.909
Stephen, I really enjoy learning from you
because you're not only an incredible thinker,

01:34:07.909 --> 01:34:15.780
technologist, I'm sure there are many multi-hyphenate
labels I could apply, but you're a very gifted

01:34:15.780 --> 01:34:17.100
communicator and teacher.

01:34:17.100 --> 01:34:22.929
So, the practical impact of what you do is
not just manifested through the products used

01:34:22.929 --> 01:34:27.500
by millions of people and that will be used,
in some form or another, by many, many millions

01:34:27.500 --> 01:34:36.070
more, but also in the principled and systematic
thinking that you can share and do share with

01:34:36.070 --> 01:34:42.140
people, including kids, including with non-technical
muggles, who are nonetheless very curious,

01:34:42.140 --> 01:34:47.140
like myself and no doubt with many, many millions
of listeners on this podcast.

01:34:47.140 --> 01:34:48.140
So thank you.

01:34:48.140 --> 01:34:52.710
I really, really appreciate the time you take
to do what you do and the time you've also

01:34:52.710 --> 01:34:54.330
taken to have this conversation.

01:34:54.330 --> 01:34:55.330
Stephen Wolfram: Thank you.

01:34:55.330 --> 01:34:57.140
Tim Ferriss: So, thank you very much.

